PI reusing the existing status-computation functions (_status_row, _read_last_activity, _heartbeat_age_seconds etc.) rather than re-implementing them: GET /api/movements (grouped list), GET /api/movements/<id> (detail: full .nexus/approved_task.json acceptance criteria, complete relay entry history in order with actor+marker+timestamp+body, git worktree status --porcelain parsed into a human label, ahead/behind origin/main counts, open PR number+state+CI conclusion via `gh pr list --head <branch>` if `gh` is available -- degrade gracefully to 'n/a' if not, never crash), GET /api/movements/<id>/log (tail of engineer.summary.log, or engineer.log if the summary doesn't exist yet, capped at a reasonable byte count), GET/PUT /api/config (currently just {\\\"poll_interval_seconds\\\": N}, persisted to a small JSON file e.g. .nexus/dashboard_config.json at the repo root -- not per-worktree). (B) Frontend refresh: plain polling via fetch() at the UI-configurable interval is sufficient and simplest (avoid SSE/WebSocket complexity unless the engineer session judges it trivial with stdlib alone -- do not add a dependency for this). (C) Real per-movement 'stage' string (addresses a gap this Product Owner already found in the existing terminal watch): derive from git worktree status instead of trusting heartbeat_at alone -- e.g. 'not started yet' (only SESSION_START posted, no worktree changes), 'developing' (uncommitted changes present), 'resolving merge conflict' (UU/AA porcelain markers), 'ready to push' (committed, no PR yet), 'PR open, CI running/red/green' (via gh), 'merged' (relay CLOSED + PR merged). (D) heartbeat_at itself: either make it genuinely periodic (the spawned engineer process or a lightweight orchestrator-side poller updates it on a real signal) or rename/relabel it honestly in both the API and the UI so it is never presented as liveness -- pid_alive stays the authoritative liveness check either way. (E) Grouping in the UI: active (phase running, pid_alive true) on top; done-but-recent (phase done, e.g. within the last N hours or last N items) in the middle; closed/merged in a collapsed-by-default bottom section -- exactly the three-tier grouping the Product Owner asked for. (F) Detail view tabs/sections: Overview (objective, acceptance criteria, current stage, branch, worktree path, PR link), Relay history (the full ordered marker log -- this is the audit trail GOV.PO.3 already relies on, so surfacing it in the UI is pure value, no new data), Log (live-tailing engineer.summary.log with fallback to raw engineer.log). (G) Read-only by default, mirroring 'orchestrator watch' and 'local_relay.py watch' precedent -- no start/stop/resume/merge action buttons in this first version; that would be a new write surface crossing back into the two-touchpoint governance boundary (GOV.PO.3 section on human interaction points) and should be its own, separately-decided follow-up if ever wanted, not bundled in silently here.\\n\\nOUT OF SCOPE for this movement (flag anything that looks like it needs these, do not build them here): authentication/multi-user support (single local user, no need); a build toolchain/npm dependency; write/action buttons (see (G)); mobile responsiveness beyond basic usability; historical analytics/charts beyond the three-tier list. ACCEPTANCE-SHAPE GUIDANCE for whoever turns this into a formal SESSION_START: reproduce the existing terminal watch's exact data shape as the JSON API's baseline (backward compatible, AC-5-style), add targeted tests for the new stage-derivation function and the config read/write endpoint in isolation (no need to test the frontend JS with a browser harness -- test the data/API layer, same testing philosophy relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013 used for its own watch loop).\\n\\n=== AMENDMENT 2026-09-08 (supersedes item (G) 'read-only, no action buttons' above; the rest of the original architecture research still stands as background) ===\\nProduct Owner-directed correction after external design review: a monitoring-only dashboard is not enough. What's actually needed is an interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench that surfaces what is expected of the Product Owner, routes the Product Owner's answer to the correct session, and lets the work continue from the same screen. Reading the 'two-touchpoint' principle (work selection, design decisions) as 'therefore read-only UI' was wrong -- work-selection and decision buttons ARE the interface for those exact two touchpoints, and a screen that lets the Product Owner change the poll interval persistently is already not purely read-only anyway. The scope correction below, written as the amendment to hand directly to the implementing engineer session (English, verbatim):\\n\\nAmend orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app from a monitoring-only dashboard to an interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench.\\n\\nPreserve the existing local stdlib backend and vanilla JS frontend. Reuse the orchestrator and local relay as the execution and decision sources; do not introduce another scheduler or independent state model.\\n\\nFirst delivery must include:\\n- A human-action inbox, ranked by blocked work and urgency.\\n- Concurrent movement cards with process status, explicit work stage, last observed activity, owner, and next action.\\n- Movement detail: acceptance criteria, conversation, events, diffs, tests, artifacts, worktree/session references, and preview links.\\n- Start/resume of authorized work, queued messages to the correct session, and observable message-delivery acknowledgments.\\n- Structured execution requests with action ID, exact operation, working directory, expected effects, and blocker source.\\n- Automatic routing of already-authorized engineering actions away from the PO role; ask the human only for an actual missing decision.\\n- Serialized integration visibility and existing authorized auto-merge.\\n\\nDo not infer work stage from Git dirtiness or heartbeat. Do not claim access to unavailable agent transcripts or hidden reasoning. UI actions must use existing authorization rules, reject stale requests, and prevent duplicate execution. Platform denials remain explicit.\\n\\nAcceptance demonstration: Track five concurrent movements; answer a pending design question; deliver a message to the correct worker; route an authorized command request; resume the affected work; open its produced UI/test artifact. Complete this flow without manual chat-to-chat copying or Git commands.\\n\\nDETAILED LAYOUT (from the same design review, informative -- the implementing session may adapt presentation but should preserve the five sections and their stated actions):\\nMain screen, five sections top to bottom: (1) 'Awaiting you' -- decisions, permission blocks, design questions; which work each one is blocking; actions: answer, approve an option, defer, reject. (2) 'Running movements' -- the concurrent movements, owner, real stage, last progress, next step; actions: open detail, send a message, request pause, stop. (3) 'PO agenda' -- suggested NEXT, ready work, dependencies, why each is waiting; actions: start, prioritize, queue. (4) 'Integration queue' -- PRs awaiting review/test/merge and why each is waiting; actions: view findings, request a fix. (5) 'Recently completed / archive' -- what shipped, test result, PR, remaining debt if any; actions: open the result and the artifact/UI that was built. A small summary strip above all five: e.g. '5 running - 2 awaiting decision - 1 in integration.' Any movement that needs a decision must surface at the TOP of its group, not just get sorted into 'active' generically.\\n\\nDetail panel (opens on click, right-hand pane), six tabs: Summary (what's being done, user-facing benefit, acceptance criteria, next step); Live activity (the agent's own posted progress notes, running command/test, latest result); Conversation (PO/engineer/reviewer messages, threaded, showing which message answered which); Changes (changed files, diff summary, branch/worktree, PR link); Evidence (test result, screenshot, render output, local UI preview); History (dispatch, correction, decision, retry, and integration events in order).\\n\\n'Where is Claude actually running' must be answerable via the relation agent/session -> movement -> worktree -> current activity -- surfaced explicitly, not hidden. If the platform does not expose it, never fabricate a full terminal transcript or hidden reasoning trace to fill the gap -- posted messages and tool events are what's real and what should be shown.\\n\\nThe single most valuable feature: an executable-request card. Convert a 'I can't run this in a terminal, no permission' message into a card shaped like: 'Test run blocked / Movement: .../ Requested action: .../ Target environment/worktree: .../ Expected effect: .../ Blocker source: PO role restriction / missing dependency / platform policy' with buttons [Route to engineer] [Run the authorized action] [Reject]. Three distinct cases must be told apart: (1) the action is already authorized but reached the wrong role -- the orchestrator routes it to the engineer automatically, no question surfaces to the Product Owner at all; (2) a genuinely new human decision is needed -- show the action and its effect, record the choice to the relay, let the affected work continue; (3) a platform/organizational policy is the actual blocker -- show the real blocker; a UI button must never be presented as if it can override that policy. The button must send a registered action id, not free-form shell text typed in the browser -- the backend resolves the id to its already-recorded operation, argument set, and working directory, which is what prevents running in the wrong worktree and prevents a double-click from starting the same thing twice; if the underlying command has changed since the id was issued, the stale approval must not apply.\\n\\nLive stage must never be inferred from git dirtiness alone: a dirty worktree does not mean 'writing code' and a clean one does not mean 'done'. Show three distinct fields -- Process status (running / exited / disconnected), Work stage (review / coding / testing / awaiting PO / integration), Last observed activity (e.g. 'test started 90s ago') -- with stage sourced from real orchestrator/engineer-posted events, heartbeat limited to meaning process reachability only, and 'last activity unknown' shown honestly when there is no evidence, never a fabricated progress percentage.\\n\\nA message box is itself part of the work: every movement needs a 'send a note to this work' field, and the message's own delivery state must be visible: Saved -> delivered to session -> received by agent -> answered. If delivery to the currently-running session isn't supported, queue it for the next continuation/resume call and say so explicitly in the UI -- never show 'Sent' for a message the agent never actually read.\\n\\nSecurity note carried over from the architecture review: once action buttons exist, localhost-only binding is not enough by itself -- add a session token plus Origin/CSRF checking, specifically so another web page open in the same browser cannot silently send commands to the local service without an extra confirmation screen.\\n\\nExplicitly deferred past the first delivery: a fully embedded terminal, desktop packaging, a drag-and-drop workflow editor, and advanced charting -- none of those block the acceptance demonstration above.\\n\\nSuccess criterion for this whole screen, stated by the Product Owner directly: 'I understood what work is waiting on me, I gave the answer it needed, and I saw the work continue on the same screen.'\\n\\n=== PO SCOPE BOUNDARY 2026-09-08 (decided, not merely flagged -- Product Owner asked for a resolved, dispatch-ready item that will not need another design round; consulting the external reviewer ('Astra') again remains an option later, but is not required to proceed) ===\\nThis backlog item's FIRST DISPATCH is bounded to the following. Anything not listed here is explicitly deferred (see 'DEFERRED' below), not silently included.\\n\\nIN SCOPE, decided:\\n- Read+relay-write only. Every card/button in this first delivery (decision inbox, message box, structured executable-request card) ends by appending an entry to the movement's own relay file (a RELAY_DECISION, a queued note, or an equivalent structured instruction) -- none of them execute git/gh/shell directly from the dashboard backend. The existing, already-audited engineer dispatch path (orchestrator start / resume, gated by scripts/nexus_engineer_tool_gate.py) is what actually executes anything, exactly as it does today for a PO decision typed into a relay append. This closes the authorization-duplication risk: there is exactly one place permission logic lives.\\n- 'Automatic routing of an already-authorized action' and the 'Run the authorized action' button are ONE mechanism, not two: both resolve to 'append this instruction to the relay, so the next dispatch/resume picks it up.' Build it once.\\n- The five-section main screen, six-tab detail panel, decision inbox ranked by urgency, movement cards with process-status/work-stage/last-activity as three distinct fields (never inferred from git dirtiness alone, never a fabricated progress percentage), and the structured executable-request card shape -- all as specified in the AMENDMENT section above -- are IN SCOPE for this first dispatch.\\n- Message-delivery UI must default its copy to the state that is actually achievable today: 'Saved -> queued for next resume' as the normal, expected outcome -- not 'delivered to session' or 'received by agent', which require a live session-control channel this repository does not have (confirmed: orchestrator.py's --resume is a fresh respawn into the same worktree, not a reconnect to a running claude session -- relay/NXS-LOCAL-0015's own finding, 2026-09-08). Do not build or imply a live-delivery guarantee.\\n- Local stdlib backend + vanilla JS frontend, orchestrator/relay as the only state and decision sources, localhost-bound. Session token + Origin/CSRF checking is IN SCOPE for this first dispatch specifically because relay-write buttons already exist here (writing a RELAY_DECISION is a real action, not merely a read) -- do not defer this to a later 'once we add real action buttons' phase; that phase is now.\\n\\nDEFERRED, explicitly out of scope for this dispatch (their own future backlog items if wanted, not part of this one):\\n- Any button that directly executes git/gh/shell from the dashboard backend itself (the earlier draft's 'Run the authorized action' as literal execution) -- stays relay-write-only per above until a specific, separately-authorized follow-up revisits it.\\n- The 'Evidence' tab's real content (screenshots, render output, local UI preview) -- ship the tab structurally (so the layout is future-proof) but it is allowed to show 'no artifact producer configured yet' rather than fabricated or empty-implying-broken content; wiring real screenshots/render output needs its own engineer-side output-contract addition (e.g. a documented .nexus/artifacts/ convention) first, not part of this dispatch.\\n- A fully embedded terminal, desktop packaging, drag-and-drop workflow editor, advanced charting (already deferred in the AMENDMENT above; restated here for a single source of truth).\\n- Any true live-session message channel (would require the orchestrator to persist and reconnect to a real claude session id -- a separate, larger movement if ever pursued; NOT bundled here).\\n\\nThis boundary is the final word for a first dispatch; the implementing engineer session should treat 'IN SCOPE' as build-it and 'DEFERRED' as report-if-tempted-to-build-it-anyway-via-RELAY_QUESTION, not as open questions requiring another Product Owner design round.\"\n}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
