{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U9dovuqWGBhNhmjzAf96TB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# GOV.PO.1 — local file-based relay protocol\n2\t\n3\t## Status\n4\t\n5\t**DRAFT — not ratified.** Implemented under `GOV_PO_1_LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL`\n6\t(relay `ozandurmus/nexus-agent-relay#15`), filed directly from an\n7\tinteractive `nexus-po` session at the Product Owner's explicit request.\n8\tPromoting this mechanism beyond DRAFT — e.g. making it the required\n9\tdefault over the GitHub-based relay — is an explicit future `DECIDE`\n10\tquestion, not decided here (see \"Non-supersession\" below). A\n11\t`nexus-decision-council` `REVIEW` round is recommended once relay#14's own\n12\tgate fix has proven itself, before this mechanism is used for anything\n13\tbeyond the small, tightly-coupled gate-fix-scale movements it was built\n14\tfor.\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Purpose\n17\t\n18\t`docs/design/NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` (\"GOV.RELAY.1\") exists because\n19\ttwo agent sessions frequently cannot read one another directly, and its\n20\tGitHub-issue transport is the right tool when that is genuinely true:\n21\tcross-machine work, a cloud-hosted agent, or anything a human outside the\n22\tcurrent machine needs to see. For a run of small, tightly-coupled\n23\tsame-machine movements — a Product Owner session and an engineer session\n24\tboth running against the same checkout — routing every intermediate\n25\texchange through a GitHub issue, `gh` authorization and network round-trips\n26\tis overhead the two sessions do not need: they can already read each\n27\tother's output through the one thing they truly share, the filesystem.\n28\t\n29\tThis document defines an **additional** transport for exactly that case:\n30\tone JSON file per movement, tracked in git, exchanged by direct read/write\n31\tagainst the shared checkout instead of `gh issue`/`gh pr` calls. It reuses\n32\t`GOV.RELAY.1`'s marker vocabulary and `GOV.SESSION.1`'s\n33\t`SESSION_START`/`SESSION_CLOSE` report schema **verbatim** — this is a new\n34\ttransport for an unchanged vocabulary, not a new vocabulary.\n35\t\n36\t## 2. Non-supersession\n37\t\n38\tThis protocol does **not** amend, deprecate, or replace\n39\t`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`, `.github/prompts/relay-bootstrap.prompt.md`,\n40\tor the `RELAY_READY owner/repository#issue` workflow. Those stay exactly as\n41\tthey are, for exactly the cases they already serve: cross-machine\n42\tcoordination, a cloud-hosted engineer or PO session, or any exchange a\n43\thuman outside this machine needs to see without being handed a local file.\n44\tA session receiving `RELAY_READY owner/repository#issue` follows the\n45\tGitHub protocol; a session receiving a local relay file path (or told to\n46\tcheck `relay/`) follows this one. Neither locator implies the other.\n47\t\n48\tRetroactively migrating relay `#11`–`#14`'s existing GitHub history into\n49\tthis format is explicitly out of scope. This slice never modifies\n50\t`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` itself.\n51\t\n52\t## 3. File shape\n53\t\n54\tOne tracked JSON file per movement under `relay/`, named\n55\t`relay/NXS-LOCAL-<4-digit-id>-<slug>.json` — e.g.\n56\t`relay/NXS-LOCAL-0001-example-movement.json`. `relay/` is a normal,\n57\tgit-tracked directory (not gitignored, not a scratch/temp pattern): these\n58\tfiles are durable governance history, the same category as\n59\t`project/*.json`, never ephemeral packet staging.\n60\t\n61\t```json\n62\t{\n63\t  \"schema_version\": 1,\n64\t  \"id\": \"NXS-LOCAL-0001\",\n65\t  \"movement\": \"EXAMPLE_MOVEMENT\",\n66\t  \"refs\": [\"docs/design/SOME_CONTRACT.md\"],\n67\t  \"status\": \"AWAITING_ENGINEER\",\n68\t  \"next_actor\": \"engineer\",\n69\t  \"created_at\": \"2026-09-08T09:00:00Z\",\n70\t  \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-08T09:00:00Z\",\n71\t  \"entries\": [\n72\t    { \"seq\": 1, \"marker\": \"SESSION_START\", \"actor\": \"po\",\n73\t      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T09:00:00Z\", \"report\": { \"...\": \"...\" } }\n74\t  ]\n75\t}\n76\t```\n77\t\n78\t- `id` — the assigned sequential id, matching the filename. Assigned by the\n79\t  tool (`create`), never chosen by the caller: it scans `relay/` for the\n80\t  highest existing `NXS-LOCAL-NNNN` id and takes the next one.\n81\t- `movement` — a short, non-empty movement identifier, the same role\n82\t  `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`'s `movement` field plays.\n83\t- `refs` — repository-relative paths to authoritative documents for this\n84\t  movement (may be empty; existence is not verified, exactly like\n85\t  `GOV.SESSION.1`'s own `refs`).\n86\t- `status` — one of `OPEN`, `IN_PROGRESS`, `AWAITING_PO`,\n87\t  `AWAITING_ENGINEER`, `CLOSED` (§5).\n88\t- `next_actor` — `\"po\"`, `\"engineer\"`, or `null` (only when `status` is\n89\t  `CLOSED`). This is the turn-ownership field (§6) — the single-writer\n90\t  mechanism the Product Owner asked for.\n91\t- `entries` — append-only. Never rewritten or reordered; a correction is\n92\t  itself a new `RELAY_CORRECTION` entry (§4), exactly as\n93\t  `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §6 already requires for the GitHub\n94\t  transport.\n95\t\n96\t## 4. Entry shape and marker vocabulary\n97\t\n98\tEvery entry carries `seq` (1-based, strictly increasing, matching array\n99\tposition), `marker`, `actor` (`\"po\"` or `\"engineer\"` — who wrote this\n100\tentry), and `timestamp` (UTC, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`). The marker vocabulary\n101\tis **exactly** `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`'s five markers plus the two\n102\t`GOV.SESSION.1` packet types — seven total, closed:\n103\t\n104\t| Marker | Extra required fields | Meaning (unchanged from the GitHub protocol) |\n105\t| --- | --- | --- |\n106\t| `SESSION_START` | `report` (`GOV.SESSION.1` `SESSION_START` schema, verbatim) | The movement's opening packet. Only legal as `entries[0]`; written by `create`, never by `append`. |\n107\t| `SESSION_CLOSE` | `report` (`GOV.SESSION.1` `SESSION_CLOSE` schema, verbatim), `outcome` | The movement's final packet. Only legal as the last entry; sets `next_actor` to `null` and `status` to `CLOSED` unconditionally. |\n108\t| `RELAY_ACK` | `subject`, `text` | Receipt/structural-validation result; never approval. |\n109\t| `RELAY_NOTE` | `subject`, `text` | Factual status or evidence; never a decision. |\n110\t| `RELAY_QUESTION` | `subject`, `text` | One material question blocking dependent work until answered. |\n111\t| `RELAY_DECISION` | `subject`, `text`, `authorized_by`, `scope`, `supersedes` | Authoritative Product Owner direction only — the same three fields `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §1 requires for an agent-published decision, required and validated here as real structured fields rather than free-text convention. |\n112\t| `RELAY_CORRECTION` | `subject`, `text` | Identifies malformed, stale, or incorrect prior material and its replacement; never deletes the entry it corrects. |\n113\t\n114\t`subject` is a short non-empty string (the marker's \"first line\" in the\n115\tGitHub protocol's grammar); `text` is the concise English body. Every entry\n116\tmay additionally carry `good_to_go: true|false` (§7).\n117\t\n118\tStructural rules `validate` enforces, mirroring `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`\n119\t§3's canonical GitHub shape:\n120\t\n121\t- `entries[0].marker` must be `SESSION_START`.\n122\t- At most one `SESSION_START`, and only at position 0.\n123\t- At most one `SESSION_CLOSE`, and only as the last entry.\n124\t- Every other entry's marker is one of the five relay markers.\n125\t- `RELAY_DECISION` always carries `authorized_by`/`scope`/`supersedes`,\n126\t  matching `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §1's requirement for an\n127\t  agent-published decision to trace to a dated written Product Owner\n128\t  instruction — the same discipline, now a validated field instead of\n129\t  parsed prose.\n130\t- `next_actor`/`status` are internally consistent with the last entry\n131\t  (§5) — a hand-edited file that violates this fails `validate`.\n132\t\n133\t## 5. Status vocabulary\n134\t\n135\t`status` is derived and persisted by the tool on every `create`/`append`,\n136\tnever hand-set independently of `next_actor`:\n137\t\n138\t- `OPEN` — exactly one entry (`SESSION_START`), nobody has responded yet.\n139\t- `AWAITING_PO` — `next_actor == \"po\"`.\n140\t- `AWAITING_ENGINEER` — `next_actor == \"engineer\"` and more than one entry\n141\t  exists (the PO has handed off at least once).\n142\t- `IN_PROGRESS` — set only via an explicit `append --in-progress` flag: the\n143\t  actor who now holds the turn (`next_actor` after this append) is actively\n144\t  working, not idly awaiting the other side's reply. Orthogonal to\n145\t  `next_actor` — it does not change who may write next, only how a human\n146\t  or session skimming `status` should read the current wait.\n147\t- `CLOSED` — `next_actor` is `null` (a `SESSION_CLOSE` or a closing entry;\n148\t  see §6).\n149\t\n150\t## 6. Turn ownership\n151\t\n152\t`next_actor` is enforced by the tool itself (`local_relay.py append`), not\n153\tmerely documented convention: an `append --role <r>` call is rejected with\n154\ta non-zero exit when `<r>` does not equal the file's current `next_actor`.\n155\tThis is the actual point of the mechanism — removing ambiguity about whose\n156\tturn it is without a human re-explaining state in chat.\n157\t\n158\t- `create` always sets `next_actor` to the role the creator is *not* — in\n159\t  the normal flow, the Product Owner creates and `next_actor` becomes\n160\t  `\"engineer\"`.\n161\t- Every `append` other than `SESSION_CLOSE` must state `--next {po|engineer}`\n162\t  explicitly, which becomes the new `next_actor`. This is a deliberate\n163\t  design choice over \"every append auto-flips the turn\": a real exchange\n164\t  is not strict ping-pong (an engineer's `RELAY_ACK` on receiving a\n165\t  `SESSION_START` does not hand the turn back to the PO — the engineer\n166\t  keeps it and starts implementing), so the appender states who goes next\n167\t  rather than the tool guessing from the marker alone.\n168\t- `--close` may be passed with any marker except `SESSION_START` (there is\n169\t  nothing to close yet) to set `next_actor` to `null` and `status` to\n170\t  `CLOSED` regardless of `--next` — generalizing the packet's \"`SESSION_CLOSE`\n171\t  or closing `RELAY_DECISION`\" phrasing to any marker, for parity with the\n172\t  GitHub transport's own `GOV_PO_1_GATE_4` precedent (`gh issue close`\n173\t  accepts an inline comment carrying any of the five markers, not only\n174\t  `RELAY_DECISION`).\n175\t- `SESSION_CLOSE` always closes unconditionally (`--next`/`--close` are\n176\t  rejected as redundant with it).\n177\t\n178\t**Concurrency.** Two processes editing the same file without a real lock\n179\tcould race if a human runs both a PO and an engineer session genuinely\n180\tconcurrently rather than in the described turn-taking sequence. `append`\n181\tre-reads the file's exact bytes immediately before writing and compares\n182\tthem, by hash, to what it read at the start of the call; a mismatch (the\n183\tfile changed underneath it — a legitimate concurrent write, not merely the\n184\tturn-ownership check above) fails closed with a clear message instead of\n185\tsilently overwriting the newer state. This is optimistic concurrency\n186\tcontrol, not a durable lock (`utils/control_plane_store.py`'s SQLite\n187\tadvisory locking is a different, heavier mechanism for a different\n188\tproblem — a local JSON file exchanged by two interactive sessions does not\n189\tneed it); it is sufficient for the turn-taking usage this protocol\n190\tdescribes, and insufficient for anything claiming true concurrent writers.\n191\t\n192\t## 7. The `good_to_go` fast path\n193\t\n194\tRoutine mutual approvals (\"looks right, go ahead\" / \"confirmed, proceeding\")\n195\tdo not need a full narrative entry. Any `append` may pass `--good-to-go`;\n196\twhen combined with a `RELAY_ACK`, `RELAY_NOTE`, `RELAY_QUESTION`, or\n197\t`RELAY_CORRECTION` marker, `--text` becomes optional (defaulting to `\"good\n198\tto go\"`, and `--subject` to the marker's own name) so the entire round-trip\n199\tcan be one flag. `good_to_go` is stored as `true` on the entry — this is a\n200\tconvenience marker for humans/sessions skimming the file, not a distinct\n201\teighth marker and not itself an approval: a `RELAY_DECISION` still needs\n202\tits own `authorized_by`/`scope`/`supersedes`, `good_to_go` or not.\n203\t\n204\t## 8. Notification model — honest, not automatic\n205\t\n206\tThere is no background watcher, daemon, or persistent polling service, and\n207\tthis section's honesty claim is unchanged: \"the next actor gets notified\"\n208\tstill means exactly what it already means for the GitHub transport — **the\n209\tnext invoked session reads the relevant `relay/<file>.json`'s current state\n210\tas the first step of its own episode start**, the same discipline\n211\t`AI_START_HERE.md` already requires before treating any packet's claims as\n212\ttrue. A human still starts each session and points it at the right file (or\n213\tthe PO/engineer skill prompts do, exactly as `RELAY_READY owner/repo#issue`\n214\tis a human- or skill-supplied locator today, not a push notification\n215\teither).\n216\t\n217\t`scripts/local_relay.py watch` (GOV_PO_1_LOCAL_RELAY_WATCH_COMMAND,\n218\t2026-09-08) narrows, but does not remove, the manual-re-checking friction\n219\tthat discipline implies: it is a single bounded, blocking, read-only\n220\tpolling command a session invokes explicitly — directly, or backgrounded\n221\tvia the harness's own background-process-plus-monitor mechanism where one\n222\tis available — never a persistent background service, and never triggered\n223\twithout that explicit invocation. It polls `--file`'s `next_actor` field\n224\tevery `--interval` seconds (default 20, hard floor 5) until it equals\n225\t`--for`, up to a bounded `--timeout` (default 1800 seconds, hard ceiling\n226\t3600); a caller could set a low `--interval` and the `--timeout` ceiling to\n227\tapproximate a near-continuous poll for up to an hour, which is accepted as\n228\ta deliberate consequence of the bound, not an oversight. `watch` decides\n229\tnothing: a detected `next_actor` flip is reported to the invoking session\n230\tas its exit code and the newly appended entries, which still requires the\n231\thuman's explicit written authorization before acting on any\n232\t`RELAY_DECISION`-class content per `GOV_PO_ROLE_MIGRATION.md` D2-D4/D12,\n233\tunchanged by this command.\n234\t\n235\t## 9. CLI — `scripts/local_relay.py`\n236\t\n237\tStdlib only, offline, synchronous — no network, daemon, credential, or\n238\tdevice access, mirroring `scripts/gov_session_transfer.py`'s own\n239\tconstraints exactly. Imports `scripts/gov_session_transfer.py` directly to\n240\tvalidate `SESSION_START`/`SESSION_CLOSE` `report` objects against its\n241\texisting schema (`_REPORT_SCHEMA_BY_TYPE`, `_validate_node`, `OUTCOMES`) —\n242\treused, not re-derived, so the two transports can never drift into two\n243\tdifferent ideas of what a valid report looks like.\n244\t\n245\t```\n246\tpy scripts/local_relay.py create   --start FILE|- [--dir relay] [--slug SLUG]\n247\tpy scripts/local_relay.py append   --file FILE --role {po|engineer} --marker MARKER\n248\t                                    [--subject S] [--text T]\n249\t                                    [--report FILE|-] [--outcome OUTCOME]\n250\t                                    [--authorized-by A] [--scope S] [--supersedes S]\n251\t                                    [--next {po|engineer}] [--close] [--good-to-go]\n252\t                                    [--in-progress]\n253\tpy scripts/local_relay.py status   --file FILE\n254\tpy scripts/local_relay.py validate --file FILE\n255\t```\n256\t\n257\t- `create` reads one **bare** `SESSION_START` packet object — the exact\n258\t  same shape `gov_session_transfer.py render` expects\n259\t  (`protocol_version: 2, message_type: \"SESSION_START\", movement, refs,\n260\t  report`) — from `FILE` or stdin (`-`), validates it with\n261\t  `gov_session_transfer.validate_fields` unchanged, and only on success\n262\t  assigns the next sequential id, derives a filesystem-safe slug from\n263\t  `movement` (or uses `--slug` if given), and writes\n264\t  `relay/NXS-LOCAL-<id>-<slug>.json`. Nothing is written on a validation\n265\t  failure, exactly like `gov_session_transfer.py render`. Prints the\n266\t  created path.\n267\t- `append` re-checks `next_actor` against `--role` (§6), builds the new\n268\t  entry per the marker's required-field table (§4), re-verifies the file's\n269\t  content hash immediately before writing (§6's concurrency note), and\n270\t  writes atomically (temp file + `os.replace`, the same discipline\n271\t  `utils/device_registry.py` already uses for its own document writes).\n272\t  Exit `1` on a rejected wrong-turn append, a concurrent-write conflict, or\n273\t  a schema violation; exit `2` on a usage error (bad arguments, missing\n274\t  file); exit `0` on success.\n275\t- `status` is read-only: prints `{id, movement, status, next_actor,\n276\t  entry_count, last_marker, last_actor, last_timestamp}` as one JSON line.\n277\t- `validate` prints `{\"valid\": bool, \"errors\": [...]}`, exit `0`/`1`, the\n278\t  same contract as `gov_session_transfer.py validate`.\n279\t\n280\t## 10. Security and privacy\n281\t\n282\t`relay/*.json` content is repository-facing engineering metadata, exactly\n283\tlike the GitHub transport's issue/comment bodies\n284\t(`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §7): no credential, token, raw device\n285\toutput, local identity value, management address, or other sensitive\n286\toperational value belongs in it, and the repository privacy gate\n287\t(`--repository-privacy-check`) scans it like any other tracked file. A\n288\tlocal relay file's existence, validity, or `good_to_go` flag never\n289\tauthorizes device contact, credential use, a Git operation, or a new\n290\tnetwork path by itself — exactly the same non-authorizing posture\n291\t`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §2 gives the GitHub locator.\n292\t\n293\t`create`/`append`'s `--start`/`--report` flags may reference an arbitrary\n294\tlocal file path without a scratch-pattern restriction (unlike the GitHub\n295\ttransport's `--body-file`, which is pinned to the `nexus_po_*` scratch\n296\tpattern). This is a deliberate difference, not an oversight: `--body-file`\n297\tis restricted because it posts to an externally-visible GitHub comment — a\n298\treal exfiltration surface. `relay/*.json` is a file the interactive PO role\n299\talready has direct `Edit`/`Write` access to under this same movement\n300\t(`scripts/nexus_po_tool_gate.py`'s new `relay/*.json` pattern); a\n301\t`--start`/`--report` path restriction here would restrict nothing an\n302\texisting, already-granted `Edit` call could not already accomplish by hand.\n303\t\n304\t## 11. Acceptance criteria\n305\t\n306\t- `relay/` is tracked, one JSON file per movement, id assigned by the tool.\n307\t- `entries` reuses `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`'s five markers plus\n308\t  `GOV.SESSION.1`'s two packet types verbatim; `SESSION_START`/`SESSION_CLOSE`\n309\t  `report` objects validate against the unchanged `GOV.SESSION.1` schema.\n310\t- `RELAY_DECISION` requires `authorized_by`/`scope`/`supersedes` as real,\n311\t  validated fields.\n312\t- `next_actor` is enforced by the tool: a wrong-role `append` is rejected,\n313\t  never silently applied out of order.\n314\t- The `good_to_go` fast path exists and does not weaken `RELAY_DECISION`'s\n315\t  own requirements.\n316\t- This document does not amend `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`,\n317\t  `.github/prompts/relay-bootstrap.prompt.md`, or the GitHub `RELAY_READY`\n318\t  workflow, and says so explicitly.\n319\t- No background watcher/daemon exists or is implied.\n320\t- No product/device/UI behavior changes as part of this movement.\n321\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02bbb3c2-9ec8-432c-ae72-fb22e59d93cf","uuid":"6bbd1c9c-8bf9-4a35-a848-51742071b951","timestamp":"2026-09-08T16:42:01.261Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/OzanDur/Codo/neXus-nexus-worktrees/NXS-LOCAL-0021/docs/design/LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md","content":"# GOV.PO.1 — local file-based relay protocol\n\n## Status\n\n**DRAFT — not ratified.** Implemented under `GOV_PO_1_LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL`\n(relay `ozandurmus/nexus-agent-relay#15`), filed directly from an\ninteractive `nexus-po` session at the Product Owner's explicit request.\nPromoting this mechanism beyond DRAFT — e.g. making it the required\ndefault over the GitHub-based relay — is an explicit future `DECIDE`\nquestion, not decided here (see \"Non-supersession\" below). A\n`nexus-decision-council` `REVIEW` round is recommended once relay#14's own\ngate fix has proven itself, before this mechanism is used for anything\nbeyond the small, tightly-coupled gate-fix-scale movements it was built\nfor.\n\n## 1. Purpose\n\n`docs/design/NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` (\"GOV.RELAY.1\") exists because\ntwo agent sessions frequently cannot read one another directly, and its\nGitHub-issue transport is the right tool when that is genuinely true:\ncross-machine work, a cloud-hosted agent, or anything a human outside the\ncurrent machine needs to see. For a run of small, tightly-coupled\nsame-machine movements — a Product Owner session and an engineer session\nboth running against the same checkout — routing every intermediate\nexchange through a GitHub issue, `gh` authorization and network round-trips\nis overhead the two sessions do not need: they can already read each\nother's output through the one thing they truly share, the filesystem.\n\nThis document defines an **additional** transport for exactly that case:\none JSON file per movement, tracked in git, exchanged by direct read/write\nagainst the shared checkout instead of `gh issue`/`gh pr` calls. It reuses\n`GOV.RELAY.1`'s marker vocabulary and `GOV.SESSION.1`'s\n`SESSION_START`/`SESSION_CLOSE` report schema **verbatim** — this is a new\ntransport for an unchanged vocabulary, not a new vocabulary.\n\n## 2. Non-supersession\n\nThis protocol does **not** amend, deprecate, or replace\n`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`, `.github/prompts/relay-bootstrap.prompt.md`,\nor the `RELAY_READY owner/repository#issue` workflow. Those stay exactly as\nthey are, for exactly the cases they already serve: cross-machine\ncoordination, a cloud-hosted engineer or PO session, or any exchange a\nhuman outside this machine needs to see without being handed a local file.\nA session receiving `RELAY_READY owner/repository#issue` follows the\nGitHub protocol; a session receiving a local relay file path (or told to\ncheck `relay/`) follows this one. Neither locator implies the other.\n\nRetroactively migrating relay `#11`–`#14`'s existing GitHub history into\nthis format is explicitly out of scope. This slice never modifies\n`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` itself.\n\n## 3. File shape\n\nOne tracked JSON file per movement under `relay/`, named\n`relay/NXS-LOCAL-<4-digit-id>-<slug>.json` — e.g.\n`relay/NXS-LOCAL-0001-example-movement.json`. `relay/` is a normal,\ngit-tracked directory (not gitignored, not a scratch/temp pattern): these\nfiles are durable governance history, the same category as\n`project/*.json`, never ephemeral packet staging.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"id\": \"NXS-LOCAL-0001\",\n  \"movement\": \"EXAMPLE_MOVEMENT\",\n  \"refs\": [\"docs/design/SOME_CONTRACT.md\"],\n  \"status\": \"AWAITING_ENGINEER\",\n  \"next_actor\": \"engineer\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-09-08T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-08T09:00:00Z\",\n  \"entries\": [\n    { \"seq\": 1, \"marker\": \"SESSION_START\", \"actor\": \"po\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T09:00:00Z\", \"report\": { \"...\": \"...\" } }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `id` — the assigned sequential id, matching the filename. Assigned by the\n  tool (`create`), never chosen by the caller: it scans `relay/` for the\n  highest existing `NXS-LOCAL-NNNN` id and takes the next one.\n- `movement` — a short, non-empty movement identifier, the same role\n  `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`'s `movement` field plays.\n- `refs` — repository-relative paths to authoritative documents for this\n  movement (may be empty; existence is not verified, exactly like\n  `GOV.SESSION.1`'s own `refs`).\n- `status` — one of `OPEN`, `IN_PROGRESS`, `AWAITING_PO`,\n  `AWAITING_ENGINEER`, `CLOSED` (§5).\n- `next_actor` — `\"po\"`, `\"engineer\"`, or `null` (only when `status` is\n  `CLOSED`). This is the turn-ownership field (§6) — the single-writer\n  mechanism the Product Owner asked for.\n- `entries` — append-only. Never rewritten or reordered; a correction is\n  itself a new `RELAY_CORRECTION` entry (§4), exactly as\n  `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §6 already requires for the GitHub\n  transport.\n\n## 4. Entry shape and marker vocabulary\n\nEvery entry carries `seq` (1-based, strictly increasing, matching array\nposition), `marker`, `actor` (`\"po\"` or `\"engineer\"` — who wrote this\nentry), and `timestamp` (UTC, `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`). The marker vocabulary\nis **exactly** `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`'s five markers plus the two\n`GOV.SESSION.1` packet types — seven total, closed:\n\n| Marker | Extra required fields | Meaning (unchanged from the GitHub protocol) |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `SESSION_START` | `report` (`GOV.SESSION.1` `SESSION_START` schema, verbatim) | The movement's opening packet. Only legal as `entries[0]`; written by `create`, never by `append`. |\n| `SESSION_CLOSE` | `report` (`GOV.SESSION.1` `SESSION_CLOSE` schema, verbatim), `outcome` | The movement's final packet. Only legal as the last entry; sets `next_actor` to `null` and `status` to `CLOSED` unconditionally. |\n| `RELAY_ACK` | `subject`, `text` | Receipt/structural-validation result; never approval. |\n| `RELAY_NOTE` | `subject`, `text` | Factual status or evidence; never a decision. |\n| `RELAY_QUESTION` | `subject`, `text` | One material question blocking dependent work until answered. |\n| `RELAY_DECISION` | `subject`, `text`, `authorized_by`, `scope`, `supersedes` | Authoritative Product Owner direction only — the same three fields `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §1 requires for an agent-published decision, required and validated here as real structured fields rather than free-text convention. |\n| `RELAY_CORRECTION` | `subject`, `text` | Identifies malformed, stale, or incorrect prior material and its replacement; never deletes the entry it corrects. |\n\n`subject` is a short non-empty string (the marker's \"first line\" in the\nGitHub protocol's grammar); `text` is the concise English body. Every entry\nmay additionally carry `good_to_go: true|false` (§7).\n\nStructural rules `validate` enforces, mirroring `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`\n§3's canonical GitHub shape:\n\n- `entries[0].marker` must be `SESSION_START`.\n- At most one `SESSION_START`, and only at position 0.\n- At most one `SESSION_CLOSE`, and only as the last entry.\n- Every other entry's marker is one of the five relay markers.\n- `RELAY_DECISION` always carries `authorized_by`/`scope`/`supersedes`,\n  matching `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §1's requirement for an\n  agent-published decision to trace to a dated written Product Owner\n  instruction — the same discipline, now a validated field instead of\n  parsed prose.\n- `next_actor`/`status` are internally consistent with the last entry\n  (§5) — a hand-edited file that violates this fails `validate`.\n\n## 5. Status vocabulary\n\n`status` is derived and persisted by the tool on every `create`/`append`,\nnever hand-set independently of `next_actor`:\n\n- `OPEN` — exactly one entry (`SESSION_START`), nobody has responded yet.\n- `AWAITING_PO` — `next_actor == \"po\"`.\n- `AWAITING_ENGINEER` — `next_actor == \"engineer\"` and more than one entry\n  exists (the PO has handed off at least once).\n- `IN_PROGRESS` — set only via an explicit `append --in-progress` flag: the\n  actor who now holds the turn (`next_actor` after this append) is actively\n  working, not idly awaiting the other side's reply. Orthogonal to\n  `next_actor` — it does not change who may write next, only how a human\n  or session skimming `status` should read the current wait.\n- `CLOSED` — `next_actor` is `null` (a `SESSION_CLOSE` or a closing entry;\n  see §6).\n\n## 6. Turn ownership\n\n`next_actor` is enforced by the tool itself (`local_relay.py append`), not\nmerely documented convention: an `append --role <r>` call is rejected with\na non-zero exit when `<r>` does not equal the file's current `next_actor`.\nThis is the actual point of the mechanism — removing ambiguity about whose\nturn it is without a human re-explaining state in chat.\n\n- `create` always sets `next_actor` to the role the creator is *not* — in\n  the normal flow, the Product Owner creates and `next_actor` becomes\n  `\"engineer\"`.\n- Every `append` other than `SESSION_CLOSE` must state `--next {po|engineer}`\n  explicitly, which becomes the new `next_actor`. This is a deliberate\n  design choice over \"every append auto-flips the turn\": a real exchange\n  is not strict ping-pong (an engineer's `RELAY_ACK` on receiving a\n  `SESSION_START` does not hand the turn back to the PO — the engineer\n  keeps it and starts implementing), so the appender states who goes next\n  rather than the tool guessing from the marker alone.\n- `--close` may be passed with any marker except `SESSION_START` (there is\n  nothing to close yet) to set `next_actor` to `null` and `status` to\n  `CLOSED` regardless of `--next` — generalizing the packet's \"`SESSION_CLOSE`\n  or closing `RELAY_DECISION`\" phrasing to any marker, for parity with the\n  GitHub transport's own `GOV_PO_1_GATE_4` precedent (`gh issue close`\n  accepts an inline comment carrying any of the five markers, not only\n  `RELAY_DECISION`).\n- `SESSION_CLOSE` always closes unconditionally (`--next`/`--close` are\n  rejected as redundant with it).\n\n**Concurrency.** Two processes editing the same file without a real lock\ncould race if a human runs both a PO and an engineer session genuinely\nconcurrently rather than in the described turn-taking sequence. `append`\nre-reads the file's exact bytes immediately before writing and compares\nthem, by hash, to what it read at the start of the call; a mismatch (the\nfile changed underneath it — a legitimate concurrent write, not merely the\nturn-ownership check above) fails closed with a clear message instead of\nsilently overwriting the newer state. This is optimistic concurrency\ncontrol, not a durable lock (`utils/control_plane_store.py`'s SQLite\nadvisory locking is a different, heavier mechanism for a different\nproblem — a local JSON file exchanged by two interactive sessions does not\nneed it); it is sufficient for the turn-taking usage this protocol\ndescribes, and insufficient for anything claiming true concurrent writers.\n\n## 7. The `good_to_go` fast path\n\nRoutine mutual approvals (\"looks right, go ahead\" / \"confirmed, proceeding\")\ndo not need a full narrative entry. Any `append` may pass `--good-to-go`;\nwhen combined with a `RELAY_ACK`, `RELAY_NOTE`, `RELAY_QUESTION`, or\n`RELAY_CORRECTION` marker, `--text` becomes optional (defaulting to `\"good\nto go\"`, and `--subject` to the marker's own name) so the entire round-trip\ncan be one flag. `good_to_go` is stored as `true` on the entry — this is a\nconvenience marker for humans/sessions skimming the file, not a distinct\neighth marker and not itself an approval: a `RELAY_DECISION` still needs\nits own `authorized_by`/`scope`/`supersedes`, `good_to_go` or not.\n\n## 8. Notification model — honest, not automatic\n\nThere is no background watcher, daemon, or persistent polling service, and\nthis section's honesty claim is unchanged: \"the next actor gets notified\"\nstill means exactly what it already means for the GitHub transport — **the\nnext invoked session reads the relevant `relay/<file>.json`'s current state\nas the first step of its own episode start**, the same discipline\n`AI_START_HERE.md` already requires before treating any packet's claims as\ntrue. A human still starts each session and points it at the right file (or\nthe PO/engineer skill prompts do, exactly as `RELAY_READY owner/repo#issue`\nis a human- or skill-supplied locator today, not a push notification\neither).\n\n`scripts/local_relay.py watch` (GOV_PO_1_LOCAL_RELAY_WATCH_COMMAND,\n2026-09-08) narrows, but does not remove, the manual-re-checking friction\nthat discipline implies: it is a single bounded, blocking, read-only\npolling command a session invokes explicitly — directly, or backgrounded\nvia the harness's own background-process-plus-monitor mechanism where one\nis available — never a persistent background service, and never triggered\nwithout that explicit invocation. It polls `--file`'s `next_actor` field\nevery `--interval` seconds (default 20, hard floor 5) until it equals\n`--for`, up to a bounded `--timeout` (default 1800 seconds, hard ceiling\n3600); a caller could set a low `--interval` and the `--timeout` ceiling to\napproximate a near-continuous poll for up to an hour, which is accepted as\na deliberate consequence of the bound, not an oversight. `watch` decides\nnothing: a detected `next_actor` flip is reported to the invoking session\nas its exit code and the newly appended entries, which still requires the\nhuman's explicit written authorization before acting on any\n`RELAY_DECISION`-class content per `GOV_PO_ROLE_MIGRATION.md` D2-D4/D12,\nunchanged by this command.\n\n## 9. CLI — `scripts/local_relay.py`\n\nStdlib only, offline, synchronous — no network, daemon, credential, or\ndevice access, mirroring `scripts/gov_session_transfer.py`'s own\nconstraints exactly. Imports `scripts/gov_session_transfer.py` directly to\nvalidate `SESSION_START`/`SESSION_CLOSE` `report` objects against its\nexisting schema (`_REPORT_SCHEMA_BY_TYPE`, `_validate_node`, `OUTCOMES`) —\nreused, not re-derived, so the two transports can never drift into two\ndifferent ideas of what a valid report looks like.\n\n```\npy scripts/local_relay.py create   --start FILE|- [--dir relay] [--slug SLUG]\npy scripts/local_relay.py append   --file FILE --role {po|engineer} --marker MARKER\n                                    [--subject S] [--text T]\n                                    [--report FILE|-] [--outcome OUTCOME]\n                                    [--authorized-by A] [--scope S] [--supersedes S]\n                                    [--next {po|engineer}] [--close] [--good-to-go]\n                                    [--in-progress]\npy scripts/local_relay.py status   --file FILE\npy scripts/local_relay.py validate --file FILE\n```\n\n- `create` reads one **bare** `SESSION_START` packet object — the exact\n  same shape `gov_session_transfer.py render` expects\n  (`protocol_version: 2, message_type: \"SESSION_START\", movement, refs,\n  report`) — from `FILE` or stdin (`-`), validates it with\n  `gov_session_transfer.validate_fields` unchanged, and only on success\n  assigns the next sequential id, derives a filesystem-safe slug from\n  `movement` (or uses `--slug` if given), and writes\n  `relay/NXS-LOCAL-<id>-<slug>.json`. Nothing is written on a validation\n  failure, exactly like `gov_session_transfer.py render`. Prints the\n  created path.\n- `append` re-checks `next_actor` against `--role` (§6), builds the new\n  entry per the marker's required-field table (§4), re-verifies the file's\n  content hash immediately before writing (§6's concurrency note), and\n  writes atomically (temp file + `os.replace`, the same discipline\n  `utils/device_registry.py` already uses for its own document writes).\n  Exit `1` on a rejected wrong-turn append, a concurrent-write conflict, or\n  a schema violation; exit `2` on a usage error (bad arguments, missing\n  file); exit `0` on success.\n- `status` is read-only: prints `{id, movement, status, next_actor,\n  entry_count, last_marker, last_actor, last_timestamp}` as one JSON line.\n- `validate` prints `{\"valid\": bool, \"errors\": [...]}`, exit `0`/`1`, the\n  same contract as `gov_session_transfer.py validate`.\n\n## 10. Security and privacy\n\n`relay/*.json` content is repository-facing engineering metadata, exactly\nlike the GitHub transport's issue/comment bodies\n(`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §7): no credential, token, raw device\noutput, local identity value, management address, or other sensitive\noperational value belongs in it, and the repository privacy gate\n(`--repository-privacy-check`) scans it like any other tracked file. A\nlocal relay file's existence, validity, or `good_to_go` flag never\nauthorizes device contact, credential use, a Git operation, or a new\nnetwork path by itself — exactly the same non-authorizing posture\n`NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` §2 gives the GitHub locator.\n\n`create`/`append`'s `--start`/`--report` flags may reference an arbitrary\nlocal file path without a scratch-pattern restriction (unlike the GitHub\ntransport's `--body-file`, which is pinned to the `nexus_po_*` scratch\npattern). This is a deliberate difference, not an oversight: `--body-file`\nis restricted because it posts to an externally-visible GitHub comment — a\nreal exfiltration surface. `relay/*.json` is a file the interactive PO role\nalready has direct `Edit`/`Write` access to under this same movement\n(`scripts/nexus_po_tool_gate.py`'s new `relay/*.json` pattern); a\n`--start`/`--report` path restriction here would restrict nothing an\nexisting, already-granted `Edit` call could not already accomplish by hand.\n\n## 11. Acceptance criteria\n\n- `relay/` is tracked, one JSON file per movement, id assigned by the tool.\n- `entries` reuses `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`'s five markers plus\n  `GOV.SESSION.1`'s two packet types verbatim; `SESSION_START`/`SESSION_CLOSE`\n  `report` objects validate against the unchanged `GOV.SESSION.1` schema.\n- `RELAY_DECISION` requires `authorized_by`/`scope`/`supersedes` as real,\n  validated fields.\n- `next_actor` is enforced by the tool: a wrong-role `append` is rejected,\n  never silently applied out of order.\n- The `good_to_go` fast path exists and does not weaken `RELAY_DECISION`'s\n  own requirements.\n- This document does not amend `NEXUS_AGENT_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md`,\n  `.github/prompts/relay-bootstrap.prompt.md`, or the GitHub `RELAY_READY`\n  workflow, and says so explicitly.\n- No background watcher/daemon exists or is implied.\n- No product/device/UI behavior changes as part of this movement.\n","numLines":321,"startLine":1,"totalLines":321}}}
