{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VT8C9Aovu1vJdErFV6ep6X","type":"tool_result","content":"190\t  `console_actions.js`, and the payloads arrive over `GET /api/payloads`.\n191\t\n192\tOne runtime flag, set by the page shell, decides which world a module is in:\n193\t\n194\t```js\n195\twindow.SECURITYEXPERT_MODE = \"static\" | \"console\";   // set before any module runs\n196\t```\n197\t\n198\tThe single frontend change this requires is that report initialization becomes\n199\ta **function call** rather than top-level execution over inline constants:\n200\t`initializeReport(payloads)`. Static mode calls it with the inlined constants;\n201\tconsole mode calls it after a fetch. This is owned by `app_bootstrap.js` and is\n202\ttherefore a change to a file `codebase_modularization` is already rewriting —\n203\twhich is why **`CON.1` must not start before that build is DONE** (§10).\n204\t\n205\tInvariant: **the console never introduces a payload shape the exporter does not\n206\talso produce.** If the console needs a field, the builder gains it and both\n207\tsurfaces get it. Enforced by an equality test in `CON.1` (AC-4 there).\n208\t\n209\t## 7. Security model (hard rules)\n210\t\n211\t1. **Loopback only.** The listener binds `127.0.0.1`. Binding anything else is\n212\t   a `DEPLOY.1A`-class decision, not a flag someone flips — the same rule the\n213\t   compose file already applies to the nginx report viewer.\n214\t2. **Cookieless bearer authentication.** A token is generated per launch,\n215\t   printed once to the operator's terminal inside the URL fragment\n216\t   (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/#t=<token>`). The shell reads it from\n217\t   `location.hash`, strips it via `history.replaceState`, holds it in memory,\n218\t   and sends it as `Authorization: Bearer`. **No cookie exists**, therefore no\n219\t   ambient credential exists, therefore the class of CSRF where another local\n220\t   page drives the console is structurally impossible — not merely mitigated.\n221\t   `Origin` / `Sec-Fetch-Site` are checked as defence in depth. The fragment is\n222\t   never transmitted to the server and never lands in an access log.\n223\t3. **The shell is data-free.** `GET /` and `/assets/*` are unauthenticated\n224\t   because they contain no evidence — only repository code. Every `/api/*`\n225\t   route requires the token; comparison is constant-time.\n226\t4. **Credentials never reach the browser.** They are resolved in the engine\n227\t   process exactly as they are today. No route returns, echoes, or accepts one.\n228\t5. **The console is in the worker trust zone, never the viewer zone.** It may\n229\t   not run in, or be reachable from, the nginx viewer container. It must never\n230\t   be given the recovery volume as a served path.\n231\t6. **No recovery payload is ever reachable over HTTP.** Manifests, readiness and\n232\t   validation verdicts only — never bytes, never a download link, never a\n233\t   decrypt path (`BACKUP_AND_RECOVERY_ARCHITECTURE.md` §11, restated as a\n234\t   route-table test in `CON.4`).\n235\t7. **Three independent gates for an `operational-write`.** The UI hides a\n236\t   non-allowlisted target, the API refuses it, and the collector refuses it.\n237\t   No layer is trusted to be the only one.\n238\t8. **Job records are identity-lean.** A job record carries `entity_id`,\n239\t   job type, timing and outcome. No credential, no management address, no raw\n240\t   device output, no backup bytes. It lives under `data_root`, never under\n241\t   `output_root`, and never enters the support bundle.\n242\t9. **Audit before action.** A job record reaches durable storage in `queued`\n243\t   state before the runner may start it. A crash mid-action must leave\n244\t   evidence that the action was attempted.\n245\t10. **Device contact frequency is unchanged.** The UI polls the job store, never\n246\t    a device. Auto-refresh reads artifacts on disk. No console feature may\n247\t    cause a collector to run more often than an operator explicitly asked.\n248\t11. **The enrollment intent is bounded by §4.1** (amendment, 2026-09-05).\n249\t    Restated here as security rules because they are: the browser submits only\n250\t    the closed enrollment schema and never a credential payload, command, argv\n251\t    or path; the enrollment request performs no device I/O; first contact is a\n252\t    separate queued CLASS 0 read-only job; strict transport trust precedes\n253\t    credential submission for **every** endpoint, candidates included; identity\n254\t    requires positive evidence and ambiguity persists nothing; confirmation\n255\t    precedes persistence and an immutable audit record precedes the registry\n256\t    mutation; persistence uses the one existing `DeviceRegistry` path with its\n257\t    duplicate-detection and mutation-lock contract unchanged; and the whole\n258\t    permission is conditioned on the loopback binding, with server mode blocked\n259\t    until `DEPLOY.1A`.\n260\t\n261\t## 8. Deployment shapes\n262\t\n263\t| Shape | When | Identity | Status |\n264\t|---|---|---|---|\n265\t| **Laptop console** — `py main.py --console`, loopback, per-launch token, single operator | now (`CON.1`–`CON.5`) | the OS user who launched it | this track |\n266\t| **Server control plane** — same ASGI app behind an authenticated reverse proxy | only after the `DEPLOY.1` gates | corporate OIDC + role mapping, audited | out of scope here; blocked |\n267\t| **Report viewer** — nginx serving `output/` read-only | today | none (loopback) | unchanged; never becomes the console |\n268\t\n269\tThe application code is identical between shape 1 and shape 2. What changes is"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02bbb3c2-9ec8-432c-ae72-fb22e59d93cf","uuid":"9372b366-61a4-4a4b-83cb-6b6317ca86cb","timestamp":"2026-09-08T16:42:24.828Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/OzanDur/Codo/neXus-nexus-worktrees/NXS-LOCAL-0021/docs/design/OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md","content":"  `console_actions.js`, and the payloads arrive over `GET /api/payloads`.\n\nOne runtime flag, set by the page shell, decides which world a module is in:\n\n```js\nwindow.SECURITYEXPERT_MODE = \"static\" | \"console\";   // set before any module runs\n```\n\nThe single frontend change this requires is that report initialization becomes\na **function call** rather than top-level execution over inline constants:\n`initializeReport(payloads)`. Static mode calls it with the inlined constants;\nconsole mode calls it after a fetch. This is owned by `app_bootstrap.js` and is\ntherefore a change to a file `codebase_modularization` is already rewriting —\nwhich is why **`CON.1` must not start before that build is DONE** (§10).\n\nInvariant: **the console never introduces a payload shape the exporter does not\nalso produce.** If the console needs a field, the builder gains it and both\nsurfaces get it. Enforced by an equality test in `CON.1` (AC-4 there).\n\n## 7. Security model (hard rules)\n\n1. **Loopback only.** The listener binds `127.0.0.1`. Binding anything else is\n   a `DEPLOY.1A`-class decision, not a flag someone flips — the same rule the\n   compose file already applies to the nginx report viewer.\n2. **Cookieless bearer authentication.** A token is generated per launch,\n   printed once to the operator's terminal inside the URL fragment\n   (`http://127.0.0.1:<port>/#t=<token>`). The shell reads it from\n   `location.hash`, strips it via `history.replaceState`, holds it in memory,\n   and sends it as `Authorization: Bearer`. **No cookie exists**, therefore no\n   ambient credential exists, therefore the class of CSRF where another local\n   page drives the console is structurally impossible — not merely mitigated.\n   `Origin` / `Sec-Fetch-Site` are checked as defence in depth. The fragment is\n   never transmitted to the server and never lands in an access log.\n3. **The shell is data-free.** `GET /` and `/assets/*` are unauthenticated\n   because they contain no evidence — only repository code. Every `/api/*`\n   route requires the token; comparison is constant-time.\n4. **Credentials never reach the browser.** They are resolved in the engine\n   process exactly as they are today. No route returns, echoes, or accepts one.\n5. **The console is in the worker trust zone, never the viewer zone.** It may\n   not run in, or be reachable from, the nginx viewer container. It must never\n   be given the recovery volume as a served path.\n6. **No recovery payload is ever reachable over HTTP.** Manifests, readiness and\n   validation verdicts only — never bytes, never a download link, never a\n   decrypt path (`BACKUP_AND_RECOVERY_ARCHITECTURE.md` §11, restated as a\n   route-table test in `CON.4`).\n7. **Three independent gates for an `operational-write`.** The UI hides a\n   non-allowlisted target, the API refuses it, and the collector refuses it.\n   No layer is trusted to be the only one.\n8. **Job records are identity-lean.** A job record carries `entity_id`,\n   job type, timing and outcome. No credential, no management address, no raw\n   device output, no backup bytes. It lives under `data_root`, never under\n   `output_root`, and never enters the support bundle.\n9. **Audit before action.** A job record reaches durable storage in `queued`\n   state before the runner may start it. A crash mid-action must leave\n   evidence that the action was attempted.\n10. **Device contact frequency is unchanged.** The UI polls the job store, never\n    a device. Auto-refresh reads artifacts on disk. No console feature may\n    cause a collector to run more often than an operator explicitly asked.\n11. **The enrollment intent is bounded by §4.1** (amendment, 2026-09-05).\n    Restated here as security rules because they are: the browser submits only\n    the closed enrollment schema and never a credential payload, command, argv\n    or path; the enrollment request performs no device I/O; first contact is a\n    separate queued CLASS 0 read-only job; strict transport trust precedes\n    credential submission for **every** endpoint, candidates included; identity\n    requires positive evidence and ambiguity persists nothing; confirmation\n    precedes persistence and an immutable audit record precedes the registry\n    mutation; persistence uses the one existing `DeviceRegistry` path with its\n    duplicate-detection and mutation-lock contract unchanged; and the whole\n    permission is conditioned on the loopback binding, with server mode blocked\n    until `DEPLOY.1A`.\n\n## 8. Deployment shapes\n\n| Shape | When | Identity | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Laptop console** — `py main.py --console`, loopback, per-launch token, single operator | now (`CON.1`–`CON.5`) | the OS user who launched it | this track |\n| **Server control plane** — same ASGI app behind an authenticated reverse proxy | only after the `DEPLOY.1` gates | corporate OIDC + role mapping, audited | out of scope here; blocked |\n| **Report viewer** — nginx serving `output/` read-only | today | none (loopback) | unchanged; never becomes the console |\n\nThe application code is identical between shape 1 and shape 2. What changes is","numLines":80,"startLine":190,"totalLines":394}}}
