{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011CerJx72VhqGuseiV1Zz9f","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The AC-2 design note is posted and the relay file validates cleanly. `next_actor` is now `po` — the movement's merge_gate requires an explicit Product Owner acknowledgment before AC-3 implementation begins, so I'll stop here and wait rather than starting implementation.\n\nSummary of what I did, per AC-1/AC-2:\n1. Read `project/backlog.json`'s `orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app` entry in full (prior-art survey, recommended architecture, AMENDMENT, and the final PO SCOPE BOUNDARY).\n2. Surveyed the grounding this design needed: `docs/design/LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md` (closed 7-marker vocabulary), `scripts/orchestrator.py` (existing status/staleness patterns like `REVISION_CHANGED_SINCE_DISPATCH`, state-dir bookkeeping conventions), and `docs/design/OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md` §7.2 (this repo's own prior cookieless-bearer-token CSRF design).\n3. Posted `relay/NXS-LOCAL-0021-orchestrator-interactive-dashboard-app.json` seq 2, a `RELAY_NOTE` answering AC-2's three questions:\n   - **(a)** action-id registry: content-hashed ids tied to a `source_relay_seq` snapshot (reusing orchestrator's own staleness signal), stored as runtime bookkeeping outside the relay file, with consumed/duplicate-click and staleness checks before any relay append.\n   - **(b)** no new marker — every dashboard action maps onto the existing closed vocabulary (`RELAY_DECISION` for new decisions/declines, `RELAY_NOTE` for auto-routes and queued messages), with action metadata riding as plain text in `subject`, so no schema change is needed.\n   - **(c)** session-token/CSRF: reuse this repo's own `OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md` cookieless-bearer-token-in-URL-fragment pattern, adapted to stdlib `http.server`, with Origin checked as defense in depth.\n\nRelay `next_actor` is now `po`, awaiting acknowledgment before AC-3 (the actual implementation) begins."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":381,"cache_read_input_tokens":102031,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":381},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"02bbb3c2-9ec8-432c-ae72-fb22e59d93cf","uuid":"7b4f2a22-d02c-4a89-840c-5fa02596e5f7","timestamp":"2026-09-08T16:45:35.287Z","request_id":"req_011CerJx6CPaf6UNbBWM2Yfm"}
{"duration_api_ms":226043,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"02bbb3c2-9ec8-432c-ae72-fb22e59d93cf","total_cost_usd":0.8340000000000001,"usage":{"input_tokens":44,"cache_creation_input_tokens":78516,"cache_read_input_tokens":1580565,"output_tokens":20264,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":10886},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":78516,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":792,"cache_read_input_tokens":102031,"cache_creation_input_tokens":381,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":381},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":1020,"outputTokens":15,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.001095,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":44,"outputTokens":20264,"cacheReadInputTokens":1580565,"cacheCreationInputTokens":78516,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.8329050000000002,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":10886,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"toolu_01L3Ae9PDELCUQzYLJ92uAop","tool_input":{"file_path":"/tmp/nxs0021_ac2_note.txt","content":"(a) Action-id registry. An action_id is issued only when the backend renders an executable-request card (GET /api/movements/<id>). It is sha256(canonical_json({movement_id, relay_file, operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq}))[:16], where operation is one of {route_to_engineer, record_decision, reject} and args is the exact local_relay.py append parameters (marker/subject/text/next/authorized_by/scope/supersedes) the button will submit verbatim -- never free-form text typed in the browser. source_relay_seq = len(relay_obj[\"entries\"]) at issuance time, reusing the same signal orchestrator.py._status_row already computes for its REVISION_CHANGED_SINCE_DISPATCH check, rather than inventing a second staleness idea. Registry storage follows the existing runtime-bookkeeping pattern (outside the relay file, like orchestrator's own process records): one file per movement under NEXUS_ORCHESTRATOR_STATE_DIR, \"<movement_id>.actions.json\", mapping action_id to {operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq, issued_at, consumed_at, consumed_relay_seq}. On click: unknown id is 404; an already-consumed id is rejected (duplicate-click guard); the live relay file's current entry count is compared to source_relay_seq and a mismatch is rejected as stale (something was appended since the card was rendered -- a RELAY_CORRECTION or a newer decision). Only then does the backend call local_relay.py append with the registered args and record consumed_at/consumed_relay_seq from that call's result. local_relay.py's own content-hash optimistic-concurrency check (LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 6) remains a second, independent guard at the actual write, not replaced by this registry.\n\n(b) Relay-write schema. No new marker. LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 4 states the seven-marker vocabulary is exactly closed; adding an eighth would violate that already-written contract and this AC's own instruction not to invent an incompatible file format. Every dashboard action maps onto an existing marker: a genuinely new PO decision (AMENDMENT case 2) becomes RELAY_DECISION with authorized_by/scope/supersedes populated from the session (authorized_by = the bearer-token session's identity, scope = \"<movement_id> dashboard action\", supersedes = the prior entry's seq if this replaces one, else \"none\"); an already-authorized auto-route or a queued message-box note (case 1, and the \"send a note\" field) becomes RELAY_NOTE -- factual status/instruction, never itself a new authority; a reject on a genuinely-new-decision card becomes RELAY_DECISION recording the decline, since declining is still a PO decision. Case 3 (platform/policy block) gets no write button at all -- there is nothing to append, only the real blocker text the card already shows. The action_id/operation metadata rides as plain, greppable text inside subject (e.g. \"DASHBOARD_ACTION action_id=<id> op=<operation>\") rather than as new JSON fields, so no schema change is needed anywhere -- the engineer session already reads relay entries as free text today (the standing ENGINEER_PROMPT instruction to act on RELAY_CORRECTION/RELAY_DECISION entries), exactly like every other entry.\n\n(c) Session-token + Origin/CSRF. Reuses docs/design/OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 7.2's cookieless-bearer-token pattern directly, adapted to stdlib http.server since that document's fastapi/uvicorn stack is out of scope here per AC-6. The dashboard subcommand generates one secrets.token_urlsafe(32) per process launch, printed once to the terminal as http://127.0.0.1:<port>/#t=<token>; the frontend reads it from location.hash, strips it via history.replaceState, holds it only in memory, and sends it as an Authorization: Bearer header on every /api/* call. No cookie is ever set, so the ambient-credential class of CSRF is structurally impossible, the same reasoning that document already gives; Origin is still checked as defence in depth on every state-changing (PUT/POST) /api/* route -- rejected if present and not equal to http://127.0.0.1:<port> (absence is allowed, since the bearer token, not Origin, is the real authorization boundary). Server-side token comparison uses hmac.compare_digest, never ==. GET / and static assets stay unauthenticated (no evidence lives in them, matching that document's own rule); every /api/* route, read or write, requires the token. The token lives only in a module-level in-memory variable, regenerated on every dashboard invocation, never written to disk -- the same \"runtime bookkeeping, not durable governance history\" posture orchestrator.py's own state directory already uses.\n\nWaiting for acknowledgment before starting AC-3 implementation, per this movement's merge_gate.\n"}},{"tool_name":"Write","tool_use_id":"toolu_01DsoTBJviWCjNh3PnBFzZ6p","tool_input":{"file_path":"/Users/OzanDur/Codo/neXus-nexus-worktrees/NXS-LOCAL-0021/.nexus/nxs0021_ac2_note.txt","content":"(a) Action-id registry. An action_id is issued only when the backend renders an executable-request card (GET /api/movements/<id>). It is sha256(canonical_json({movement_id, relay_file, operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq}))[:16], where operation is one of {route_to_engineer, record_decision, reject} and args is the exact local_relay.py append parameters (marker/subject/text/next/authorized_by/scope/supersedes) the button will submit verbatim -- never free-form text typed in the browser. source_relay_seq = len(relay_obj[\"entries\"]) at issuance time, reusing the same signal orchestrator.py._status_row already computes for its REVISION_CHANGED_SINCE_DISPATCH check, rather than inventing a second staleness idea. Registry storage follows the existing runtime-bookkeeping pattern (outside the relay file, like orchestrator's own process records): one file per movement under NEXUS_ORCHESTRATOR_STATE_DIR, \"<movement_id>.actions.json\", mapping action_id to {operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq, issued_at, consumed_at, consumed_relay_seq}. On click: unknown id is 404; an already-consumed id is rejected (duplicate-click guard); the live relay file's current entry count is compared to source_relay_seq and a mismatch is rejected as stale (something was appended since the card was rendered -- a RELAY_CORRECTION or a newer decision). Only then does the backend call local_relay.py append with the registered args and record consumed_at/consumed_relay_seq from that call's result. local_relay.py's own content-hash optimistic-concurrency check (LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 6) remains a second, independent guard at the actual write, not replaced by this registry.\n\n(b) Relay-write schema. No new marker. LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 4 states the seven-marker vocabulary is exactly closed; adding an eighth would violate that already-written contract and this AC's own instruction not to invent an incompatible file format. Every dashboard action maps onto an existing marker: a genuinely new PO decision (AMENDMENT case 2) becomes RELAY_DECISION with authorized_by/scope/supersedes populated from the session (authorized_by = the bearer-token session's identity, scope = \"<movement_id> dashboard action\", supersedes = the prior entry's seq if this replaces one, else \"none\"); an already-authorized auto-route or a queued message-box note (case 1, and the \"send a note\" field) becomes RELAY_NOTE -- factual status/instruction, never itself a new authority; a reject on a genuinely-new-decision card becomes RELAY_DECISION recording the decline, since declining is still a PO decision. Case 3 (platform/policy block) gets no write button at all -- there is nothing to append, only the real blocker text the card already shows. The action_id/operation metadata rides as plain, greppable text inside subject (e.g. \"DASHBOARD_ACTION action_id=<id> op=<operation>\") rather than as new JSON fields, so no schema change is needed anywhere -- the engineer session already reads relay entries as free text today (the standing ENGINEER_PROMPT instruction to act on RELAY_CORRECTION/RELAY_DECISION entries), exactly like every other entry.\n\n(c) Session-token + Origin/CSRF. Reuses docs/design/OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 7.2's cookieless-bearer-token pattern directly, adapted to stdlib http.server since that document's fastapi/uvicorn stack is out of scope here per AC-6. The dashboard subcommand generates one secrets.token_urlsafe(32) per process launch,