{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CFC2h5URPkm3p8hfiFtaYQ","type":"tool_result","content":"780\t      \"priority\": \"P3\",\n781\t      \"target\": \"A DOCS movement in an engineering session; wording only, no criterion state or feature status change\",\n782\t      \"note\": \"Both drifts were flagged as [ASSISTANT] hypotheses in PRODUCT_DIRECTION_RECORD.md section 11 and are VERIFIED against source by the first Phase A PLAN episode (2026-09-08). Reported, deliberately not fixed here -- the PO assistant does not perform mechanical documentation work (nexus-po skill section 5). DRIFT A: project/feature_registry.json left_vertical_product_navigation.criteria[no_enrollment_affordance].label still ends 'The Administration -> Device Management second entry point (PO-NAV-1's lifecycle home) is not yet built -- deferred as a disclosed follow-up, not silently dropped.' That sentence is stale. project/build_history.json's M9 record states the entry point was built in round 2, and the source agrees: static/navigation_ui.js declares the device-management entry under the Administration root, static/console_actions.js implements consoleRefreshDeviceManagementTable() under the comment 'PO-NAV-1 -- Administration -> Device Management, the second frozen enrollment entry point', and templates/console.html ships the deviceManagementModule panel with a second open button bound to the one shared m9EnrollDialog. The entry point is console-shell-only by design (D-NAV6 shell integrity), which the navigation model's own comment states; the criterion state 'done' is correct and only the trailing sentence is wrong. DRIFT B: project/feature_registry.json failover_controlled_execution has status 'planned' and a summary ending 'implementation hard-gated, not started', while CURRENT_STATE.md and project/roadmap.json record OP.2.A/OP.2.B as IMPLEMENTED and the CP ClusterXL adapter, member session and preflight/eligibility provider as implemented, unit-tested and unwired. Source confirms checkpoint/clusterxl_capability_adapter.py, checkpoint/clusterxl_member_session.py, checkpoint/clusterxl_preflight_provider.py and utils/operate/{coordinator,authorization,eligibility}.py all exist. The five criteria states remain defensible -- nothing is reachable, no adapter is wired, no OPERATE RBAC role exists, and vendor_action_adapters covers three adapters of which one exists unwired -- so only the summary wording understates reality. Suggested replacement wording for the owning DOCS movement, to be reviewed rather than pasted: 'Vendor-independent architecture FROZEN 2026-09-04; several foundations implemented and unit-tested but structurally unreachable and unwired (no taxonomy member, unconditional DENY authorizer, no production entry point). Product execution not started.' NOTE FOR THE CONVERGENCE GATE: both drifts live in free-text label/summary fields, so project_metadata_has_no_cross_authority_contradictions cannot see them; extending that check to prose is a separate question, not part of this item.\"\n783\t    },\n784\t    {\n785\t      \"id\": \"gov_po_3_ci_privacy_gate_baseline_scoping\",\n786\t      \"category\": \"Governance / CI\",\n787\t      \"title\": \"CI's 'Repository privacy gate' step fails unconditionally on every PR -- needs the same baseline-scoping fix already shipped for the local engineer hook\",\n788\t      \"status\": \"planned\",\n789\t      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n790\t      \"target\": \"Follow-up to relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014 (GOV_PO_3_PUSH_HOOK_BASELINE_SCOPING, merged PR #127); dispatch via orchestrator start once picked up, no file overlap with orchestrator.py\",\n791\t      \"note\": \"Found live 2026-09-08 while the Product Owner was monitoring five in-flight orchestrated movements: the GitHub Actions 'validation' workflow's 'Repository privacy gate' step (python main.py --repository-privacy-check, .github/workflows/validation.yml lines ~92 and ~172) scans the full working tree and fails unconditionally on the same two pre-existing, already-accepted, build-history-immutable false positives (project/build_history.json CREDENTIAL_LITERAL, relay/NXS-LOCAL-0003-local-relay-watch-command.json CREDENTIAL_LITERAL) -- confirmed failing identically on PR #127, #128, #129, #130, #131. relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014 already solved this exact problem shape for the LOCAL engineer pre-push hook (scripts/nexus_engineer_tool_gate.py, content-fingerprint baseline matching, not line-number matching) but that fix was never applied to the CI-level invocation of the same underlying scanner (application/cli.py's --repository-privacy-check path). There is no branch protection on this repo (confirmed via gh api repos/.../branches/main/protection -> 404 'Branch not protected'), so this has not blocked any merge so far -- engineers have been self-merging under the standing relay#13 authorization regardless of the red CI badge. Left unfixed, CI shows a permanent, uninformative red 'validate' status on every future PR, which defeats CI's own purpose: a genuinely new leak would look identical to this known noise. Fix direction (a full SESSION_START packet was already drafted 2026-09-08 at /private/tmp/nexus_po_ci_privacy_gate_packet.json before the Product Owner asked to hold dispatch for a new chat session): extract relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014's baseline-comparison logic into a shared module (utils/repository_privacy.py is the natural home) used by both the local hook and the CI entry point; CI's baseline is the PR's merge-base with origin/main (verify what ref info is actually available in this repo's GitHub Actions checkout for a pull_request event, and whether .github/workflows/validation.yml's checkout step needs a fetch-depth change to compute it); a finding present in both baseline and current scan is pre-existing debt and must not fail the gate, a finding only in the current scan still fails exactly as today. This movement's own PR will likely hit the same currently-broken CI check before its fix lands -- expected and self-resolving, same precedent as relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014's own SESSION_START noted.\"\n792\t    },\n793\t    {\n794\t      \"id\": \"orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app\",\n795\t      \"category\": \"Orchestrator / Observability\",\n796\t      \"title\": \"Interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench -- human-action inbox, live movement tracking, detail panel, message delivery, authorized command routing, integration visibility (supersedes the earlier read-only-dashboard framing)\",\n797\t      \"status\": \"planned\",\n798\t      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n799\t      \"target\": \"Follow-up to relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013 (GOV_PO_3_ORCHESTRATOR_OBSERVABILITY, merged PR #129, terminal-only 'orchestrator watch'); AMENDED 2026-09-08 per Product Owner-provided design review (external architecture pass, 'Astra') before dispatch; explicitly requested by the Product Owner as the next dispatch once queued\",\n800\t      \"note\": \"Product Owner feedback 2026-09-08, given directly after 'orchestrator watch' (relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013) shipped: the terminal table is 'cok yuzeysel bir arayuz' (too superficial an interface), followed by an explicit request to research the shape of this kind of app once and land on a complete, dispatch-ready spec rather than iterating back and forth. The spec below is that research, written so a single engineering movement can implement it without a further design round; treat it as strong direction, not a rigid contract -- the engineer session may adjust implementation detail, but should flag via RELAY_QUESTION rather than silently drop anything under 'REQUIRED'. NOT YET DISPATCHED -- the Product Owner asked to queue this rather than dispatch immediately, pending a session/chat handoff.\\n\\nPRODUCT OWNER'S EXPLICIT REQUIREMENTS (verbatim intent preserved): (1) a real clickable app, not a terminal redraw loop; (2) it reads the orchestrator's own state, and the refresh interval must be changeable from inside the UI itself (persisted, not only a --interval CLI flag); (3) a list of all tasks/movements; (4) clicking one opens a full detail view; (5) at a glance: what is currently being developed, what has already been developed (done/merged), and what stage each active movement is at, grouped active-on-top / recently-done-in-the-middle / closed-at-the-bottom, not one flat list.\\n\\nPRIOR ART SURVEYED (what comparable local dev-tool dashboards do, and what to borrow): CI/job dashboards (GitHub Actions run list, Airflow DAG-run UI, Prefect/Dagster run UI) all share the same shape -- a grouped/filterable run list with a colored status badge, a click-through detail view with a tabbed layout (overview / logs / timeline), and a live-tailing log pane. Process managers (pm2, Docker Desktop/Portainer, k9s) add the 'what's alive right now' angle: liveness derived from the actual OS-level process/container check, never from a timestamp alone -- confirms this movement's own finding that pid_alive, not heartbeat_at, is the only honest liveness signal today. The consistent architectural pattern across all of them for a single-user local tool: a thin local backend that already owns the ground truth (here: scripts/orchestrator.py's state dir + relay/*.json + each worktree's git status) exposes a small read JSON API; a static single-page frontend polls or subscribes to it; no user accounts, no multi-tenancy, bind to localhost only.\\n\\nRECOMMENDED ARCHITECTURE (REQUIRED unless the engineer session finds a concrete blocker, reported via RELAY_QUESTION): (A) Backend -- a new `python3 scripts/orchestrator.py dashboard [--port N, default e.g. 8765] [--relay-dir] [--state-dir]` subcommand, stdlib-only (http.server/socketserver/wsgiref -- matches the existing 'no new third-party dependency' invariant already enforced for orchestrator.py and local_relay.py), bound to 127.0.0.1 only. It serves (a1) one static HTML/CSS/JS page (vanilla, no build step, single file or a small handful under a new scripts/dashboard_assets/ or similar -- no npm/webpack/node toolchain in this repo, do not introduce one), and (a2) a small JSON API reusing the existing status-computation functions (_status_row, _read_last_activity, _heartbeat_age_seconds etc.) rather than re-implementing them: GET /api/movements (grouped list), GET /api/movements/<id> (detail: full .nexus/approved_task.json acceptance criteria, complete relay entry history in order with actor+marker+timestamp+body, git worktree status --porcelain parsed into a human label, ahead/behind origin/main counts, open PR number+state+CI conclusion via `gh pr list --head <branch>` if `gh` is available -- degrade gracefully to 'n/a' if not, never crash), GET /api/movements/<id>/log (tail of engineer.summary.log, or engineer.log if the summary doesn't exist yet, capped at a reasonable byte count), GET/PUT /api/config (currently just {\\\"poll_interval_seconds\\\": N}, persisted to a small JSON file e.g. .nexus/dashboard_config.json at the repo root -- not per-worktree). (B) Frontend refresh: plain polling via fetch() at the UI-configurable interval is sufficient and simplest (avoid SSE/WebSocket complexity unless the engineer session judges it trivial with stdlib alone -- do not add a dependency for this). (C) Real per-movement 'stage' string (addresses a gap this Product Owner already found in the existing terminal watch): derive from git worktree status instead of trusting heartbeat_at alone -- e.g. 'not started yet' (only SESSION_START posted, no worktree changes), 'developing' (uncommitted changes present), 'resolving merge conflict' (UU/AA porcelain markers), 'ready to push' (committed, no PR yet), 'PR open, CI running/red/green' (via gh), 'merged' (relay CLOSED + PR merged). (D) heartbeat_at itself: either make it genuinely periodic (the spawned engineer process or a lightweight orchestrator-side poller updates it on a real signal) or rename/relabel it honestly in both the API and the UI so it is never presented as liveness -- pid_alive stays the authoritative liveness check either way. (E) Grouping in the UI: active (phase running, pid_alive true) on top; done-but-recent (phase done, e.g. within the last N hours or last N items) in the middle; closed/merged in a collapsed-by-default bottom section -- exactly the three-tier grouping the Product Owner asked for. (F) Detail view tabs/sections: Overview (objective, acceptance criteria, current stage, branch, worktree path, PR link), Relay history (the full ordered marker log -- this is the audit trail GOV.PO.3 already relies on, so surfacing it in the UI is pure value, no new data), Log (live-tailing engineer.summary.log with fallback to raw engineer.log). (G) Read-only by default, mirroring 'orchestrator watch' and 'local_relay.py watch' precedent -- no start/stop/resume/merge action buttons in this first version; that would be a new write surface crossing back into the two-touchpoint governance boundary (GOV.PO.3 section on human interaction points) and should be its own, separately-decided follow-up if ever wanted, not bundled in silently here.\\n\\nOUT OF SCOPE for this movement (flag anything that looks like it needs these, do not build them here): authentication/multi-user support (single local user, no need); a build toolchain/npm dependency; write/action buttons (see (G)); mobile responsiveness beyond basic usability; historical analytics/charts beyond the three-tier list. ACCEPTANCE-SHAPE GUIDANCE for whoever turns this into a formal SESSION_START: reproduce the existing terminal watch's exact data shape as the JSON API's baseline (backward compatible, AC-5-style), add targeted tests for the new stage-derivation function and the config read/write endpoint in isolation (no need to test the frontend JS with a browser harness -- test the data/API layer, same testing philosophy relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013 used for its own watch loop).\\n\\n=== AMENDMENT 2026-09-08 (supersedes item (G) 'read-only, no action buttons' above; the rest of the original architecture research still stands as background) ===\\nProduct Owner-directed correction after external design review: a monitoring-only dashboard is not enough. What's actually needed is an interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench that surfaces what is expected of the Product Owner, routes the Product Owner's answer to the correct session, and lets the work continue from the same screen. Reading the 'two-touchpoint' principle (work selection, design decisions) as 'therefore read-only UI' was wrong -- work-selection and decision buttons ARE the interface for those exact two touchpoints, and a screen that lets the Product Owner change the poll interval persistently is already not purely read-only anyway. The scope correction below, written as the amendment to hand directly to the implementing engineer session (English, verbatim):\\n\\nAmend orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app from a monitoring-only dashboard to an interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench.\\n\\nPreserve the existing local stdlib backend and vanilla JS frontend. Reuse the orchestrator and local relay as the execution and decision sources; do not introduce another scheduler or independent state model.\\n\\nFirst delivery must include:\\n- A human-action inbox, ranked by blocked work and urgency.\\n- Concurrent movement cards with process status, explicit work stage, last observed activity, owner, and next action.\\n- Movement detail: acceptance criteria, conversation, events, diffs, tests, artifacts, worktree/session references, and preview links.\\n- Start/resume of authorized work, queued messages to the correct session, and observable message-delivery acknowledgments.\\n- Structured execution requests with action ID, exact operation, working directory, expected effects, and blocker source.\\n- Automatic routing of already-authorized engineering actions away from the PO role; ask the human only for an actual missing decision.\\n- Serialized integration visibility and existing authorized auto-merge.\\n\\nDo not infer work stage from Git dirtiness or heartbeat. Do not claim access to unavailable agent transcripts or hidden reasoning. UI actions must use existing authorization rules, reject stale requests, and prevent duplicate execution. Platform denials remain explicit.\\n\\nAcceptance demonstration: Track five concurrent movements; answer a pending design question; deliver a message to the correct worker; route an authorized command request; resume the affected work; open its produced UI/test artifact. Complete this flow without manual chat-to-chat copying or Git commands.\\n\\nDETAILED LAYOUT (from the same design review, informative -- the implementing session may adapt presentation but should preserve the five sections and their stated actions):\\nMain screen, five sections top to bottom: (1) 'Awaiting you' -- decisions, permission blocks, design questions; which work each one is blocking; actions: answer, approve an option, defer, reject. (2) 'Running movements' -- the concurrent movements, owner, real stage, last progress, next step; actions: open detail, send a message, request pause, stop. (3) 'PO agenda' -- suggested NEXT, ready work, dependencies, why each is waiting; actions: start, prioritize, queue. (4) 'Integration queue' -- PRs awaiting review/test/merge and why each is waiting; actions: view findings, request a fix. (5) 'Recently completed / archive' -- what shipped, test result, PR, remaining debt if any; actions: open the result and the artifact/UI that was built. A small summary strip above all five: e.g. '5 running - 2 awaiting decision - 1 in integration.' Any movement that needs a decision must surface at the TOP of its group, not just get sorted into 'active' generically.\\n\\nDetail panel (opens on click, right-hand pane), six tabs: Summary (what's being done, user-facing benefit, acceptance criteria, next step); Live activity (the agent's own posted progress notes, running command/test, latest result); Conversation (PO/engineer/reviewer messages, threaded, showing which message answered which); Changes (changed files, diff summary, branch/worktree, PR link); Evidence (test result, screenshot, render output, local UI preview); History (dispatch, correction, decision, retry, and integration events in order).\\n\\n'Where is Claude actually running' must be answerable via the relation agent/session -> movement -> worktree -> current activity -- surfaced explicitly, not hidden. If the platform does not expose it, never fabricate a full terminal transcript or hidden reasoning trace to fill the gap -- posted messages and tool events are what's real and what should be shown.\\n\\nThe single most valuable feature: an executable-request card. Convert a 'I can't run this in a terminal, no permission' message into a card shaped like: 'Test run blocked / Movement: .../ Requested action: .../ Target environment/worktree: .../ Expected effect: .../ Blocker source: PO role restriction / missing dependency / platform policy' with buttons [Route to engineer] [Run the authorized action] [Reject]. Three distinct cases must be told apart: (1) the action is already authorized but reached the wrong role -- the orchestrator routes it to the engineer automatically, no question surfaces to the Product Owner at all; (2) a genuinely new human decision is needed -- show the action and its effect, record the choice to the relay, let the affected work continue; (3) a platform/organizational policy is the actual blocker -- show the real blocker; a UI button must never be presented as if it can override that policy. The button must send a registered action id, not free-form shell text typed in the browser -- the backend resolves the id to its already-recorded operation, argument set, and working directory, which is what prevents running in the wrong worktree and prevents a double-click from starting the same thing twice; if the underlying command has changed since the id was issued, the stale approval must not apply.\\n\\nLive stage must never be inferred from git dirtiness alone: a dirty worktree does not mean 'writing code' and a clean one does not mean 'done'. Show three distinct fields -- Process status (running / exited / disconnected), Work stage (review / coding / testing / awaiting PO / integration), Last observed activity (e.g. 'test started 90s ago') -- with stage sourced from real orchestrator/engineer-posted events, heartbeat limited to meaning process reachability only, and 'last activity unknown' shown honestly when there is no evidence, never a fabricated progress percentage.\\n\\nA message box is itself part of the work: every movement needs a 'send a note to this work' field, and the message's own delivery state must be visible: Saved -> delivered to session -> received by agent -> answered. If delivery to the currently-running session isn't supported, queue it for the next continuation/resume call and say so explicitly in the UI -- never show 'Sent' for a message the agent never actually read.\\n\\nSecurity note carried over from the architecture review: once action buttons exist, localhost-only binding is not enough by itself -- add a session token plus Origin/CSRF checking, specifically so another web page open in the same browser cannot silently send commands to the local service without an extra confirmation screen.\\n\\nExplicitly deferred past the first delivery: a fully embedded terminal, desktop packaging, a drag-and-drop workflow editor, and advanced charting -- none of those block the acceptance demonstration above.\\n\\nSuccess criterion for this whole screen, stated by the Product Owner directly: 'I understood what work is waiting on me, I gave the answer it needed, and I saw the work continue on the same screen.'\\n\\n=== PO SCOPE BOUNDARY 2026-09-08 (decided, not merely flagged -- Product Owner asked for a resolved, dispatch-ready item that will not need another design round; consulting the external reviewer ('Astra') again remains an option later, but is not required to proceed) ===\\nThis backlog item's FIRST DISPATCH is bounded to the following. Anything not listed here is explicitly deferred (see 'DEFERRED' below), not silently included.\\n\\nIN SCOPE, decided:\\n- Read+relay-write only. Every card/button in this first delivery (decision inbox, message box, structured executable-request card) ends by appending an entry to the movement's own relay file (a RELAY_DECISION, a queued note, or an equivalent structured instruction) -- none of them execute git/gh/shell directly from the dashboard backend. The existing, already-audited engineer dispatch path (orchestrator start / resume, gated by scripts/nexus_engineer_tool_gate.py) is what actually executes anything, exactly as it does today for a PO decision typed into a relay append. This closes the authorization-duplication risk: there is exactly one place permission logic lives.\\n- 'Automatic routing of an already-authorized action' and the 'Run the authorized action' button are ONE mechanism, not two: both resolve to 'append this instruction to the relay, so the next dispatch/resume picks it up.' Build it once.\\n- The five-section main screen, six-tab detail panel, decision inbox ranked by urgency, movement cards with process-status/work-stage/last-activity as three distinct fields (never inferred from git dirtiness alone, never a fabricated progress percentage), and the structured executable-request card shape -- all as specified in the AMENDMENT section above -- are IN SCOPE for this first dispatch.\\n- Message-delivery UI must default its copy to the state that is actually achievable today: 'Saved -> queued for next resume' as the normal, expected outcome -- not 'delivered to session' or 'received by agent', which require a live session-control channel this repository does not have (confirmed: orchestrator.py's --resume is a fresh respawn into the same worktree, not a reconnect to a running claude session -- relay/NXS-LOCAL-0015's own finding, 2026-09-08). Do not build or imply a live-delivery guarantee.\\n- Local stdlib backend + vanilla JS frontend, orchestrator/relay as the only state and decision sources, localhost-bound. Session token + Origin/CSRF checking is IN SCOPE for this first dispatch specifically because relay-write buttons already exist here (writing a RELAY_DECISION is a real action, not merely a read) -- do not defer this to a later 'once we add real action buttons' phase; that phase is now.\\n\\nDEFERRED, explicitly out of scope for this dispatch (their own future backlog items if wanted, not part of this one):\\n- Any button that directly executes git/gh/shell from the dashboard backend itself (the earlier draft's 'Run the authorized action' as literal execution) -- stays relay-write-only per above until a specific, separately-authorized follow-up revisits it.\\n- The 'Evidence' tab's real content (screenshots, render output, local UI preview) -- ship the tab structurally (so the layout is future-proof) but it is allowed to show 'no artifact producer configured yet' rather than fabricated or empty-implying-broken content; wiring real screenshots/render output needs its own engineer-side output-contract addition (e.g. a documented .nexus/artifacts/ convention) first, not part of this dispatch.\\n- A fully embedded terminal, desktop packaging, drag-and-drop workflow editor, advanced charting (already deferred in the AMENDMENT above; restated here for a single source of truth).\\n- Any true live-session message channel (would require the orchestrator to persist and reconnect to a real claude session id -- a separate, larger movement if ever pursued; NOT bundled here).\\n\\nThis boundary is the final word for a first dispatch; the implementing engineer session should treat 'IN SCOPE' as build-it and 'DEFERRED' as report-if-tempted-to-build-it-anyway-via-RELAY_QUESTION, not as open questions requiring another Product Owner design round.\"\n801\t    },\n802\t    {\n803\t      \"id\": \"orchestrator_background_task_exit_race\",\n804\t      \"category\": \"Orchestrator / reliability\",\n805\t      \"title\": \"An engineer's own claude -p background task can outlive its process's turn, leaving staged-but-uncommitted work when the session exits without waiting\",\n806\t      \"status\": \"planned\",\n807\t      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n808\t      \"target\": \"scripts/orchestrator.py::_spawn_engineer and/or the ENGINEER_PROMPT -- observed live during relay/NXS-LOCAL-0018 (PR #133 merge-conflict completion), 2026-09-08\",\n809\t      \"note\": \"Real bug, observed live, not hypothetical. relay/NXS-LOCAL-0018's engineer session staged its merge-conflict-resolution changes (`git add`), then ran the full pytest regression via the claude CLI's own internal background-task mechanism (the run exceeded a 120s foreground timeout and was auto-moved to the background, e.g. `Command did not complete within its 120s timeout and was moved to the background (ID: bhpt1icqs)`). The session's own turn then ended (it printed a final assistant message and returned `result (success)`) WITHOUT waiting for that background task's completion notification -- the orchestrator's process record then showed `pid_alive: false` with the relay still at `SESSION_START` (no RELAY_ACK/NOTE/QUESTION/CLOSE ever posted) and the worktree left with everything staged but not committed (`git status --short` showed all changes in the index, nothing in `git log`). Recovered this time by a plain `orchestrator start` resume, which happened to pick the work back up cleanly (no data loss), but this is not guaranteed in general -- a background task producing a result the next resumed session never sees, or resuming mid-way through an unverified test run, is a real correctness gap for the standing 'green tests -> self-merge' authorization (relay#13): a session must not be able to reach a commit/push/merge step on the strength of a background test run it never actually waited to see the result of.\\n\\nFix direction (not yet designed in detail -- needs its own investigation before a SESSION_START is written): (a) the ENGINEER_PROMPT (scripts/orchestrator.py) could be strengthened to explicitly instruct every engineer session to never treat a long-running validation command as backgroundable -- always run the full regression suite as a foreground, awaited command, even past whatever timeout the CLI would otherwise auto-background it at (this may need a per-invocation flag/env var to raise or disable that auto-background threshold for engineer sessions specifically, if one exists -- verify before assuming); (b) alternatively/additionally, the orchestrator's own resume path (decide_start) could detect this specific pattern (index has staged-but-uncommitted changes, relay never advanced past SESSION_START, pid dead) and inject an explicit instruction into the resumed session's context to first check for and finish any prior staged-but-uncommitted work and any background task results before proceeding, rather than relying on the resumed session to figure this out unprompted (it did, this time, but that's not yet a guarantee). Report via RELAY_QUESTION if investigation finds the CLI gives no way to control or query this background-task behavior from outside the session.\\n\\nNot urgent-blocking (recovery worked this time via a plain resume), but real and worth fixing before it causes an actual lost-work or premature-merge incident rather than just an extra resume cycle.\\n\\nPriority raised P1 -> P0 on 2026-09-08 per explicit Product Owner direction: this is the one concrete engineering item among this session's PO-orchestration-efficiency findings (see PRODUCT_DIRECTION_RECORD.md section 14), and the standing relay#13 'green tests -> self-merge' authorization must not remain exposed to an unverified background test result.\"\n810\t    }\n811\t  ]\n812\t}\n813\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a8dd4a7-31e7-434f-89b1-73b3ad9d3b22","uuid":"4371694e-a1e4-4430-8d87-ce9c6b61900c","timestamp":"2026-09-08T17:07:01.452Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/OzanDur/Codo/neXus-nexus-worktrees/NXS-LOCAL-0021/project/backlog.json","content":"      \"priority\": \"P3\",\n      \"target\": \"A DOCS movement in an engineering session; wording only, no criterion state or feature status change\",\n      \"note\": \"Both drifts were flagged as [ASSISTANT] hypotheses in PRODUCT_DIRECTION_RECORD.md section 11 and are VERIFIED against source by the first Phase A PLAN episode (2026-09-08). Reported, deliberately not fixed here -- the PO assistant does not perform mechanical documentation work (nexus-po skill section 5). DRIFT A: project/feature_registry.json left_vertical_product_navigation.criteria[no_enrollment_affordance].label still ends 'The Administration -> Device Management second entry point (PO-NAV-1's lifecycle home) is not yet built -- deferred as a disclosed follow-up, not silently dropped.' That sentence is stale. project/build_history.json's M9 record states the entry point was built in round 2, and the source agrees: static/navigation_ui.js declares the device-management entry under the Administration root, static/console_actions.js implements consoleRefreshDeviceManagementTable() under the comment 'PO-NAV-1 -- Administration -> Device Management, the second frozen enrollment entry point', and templates/console.html ships the deviceManagementModule panel with a second open button bound to the one shared m9EnrollDialog. The entry point is console-shell-only by design (D-NAV6 shell integrity), which the navigation model's own comment states; the criterion state 'done' is correct and only the trailing sentence is wrong. DRIFT B: project/feature_registry.json failover_controlled_execution has status 'planned' and a summary ending 'implementation hard-gated, not started', while CURRENT_STATE.md and project/roadmap.json record OP.2.A/OP.2.B as IMPLEMENTED and the CP ClusterXL adapter, member session and preflight/eligibility provider as implemented, unit-tested and unwired. Source confirms checkpoint/clusterxl_capability_adapter.py, checkpoint/clusterxl_member_session.py, checkpoint/clusterxl_preflight_provider.py and utils/operate/{coordinator,authorization,eligibility}.py all exist. The five criteria states remain defensible -- nothing is reachable, no adapter is wired, no OPERATE RBAC role exists, and vendor_action_adapters covers three adapters of which one exists unwired -- so only the summary wording understates reality. Suggested replacement wording for the owning DOCS movement, to be reviewed rather than pasted: 'Vendor-independent architecture FROZEN 2026-09-04; several foundations implemented and unit-tested but structurally unreachable and unwired (no taxonomy member, unconditional DENY authorizer, no production entry point). Product execution not started.' NOTE FOR THE CONVERGENCE GATE: both drifts live in free-text label/summary fields, so project_metadata_has_no_cross_authority_contradictions cannot see them; extending that check to prose is a separate question, not part of this item.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"gov_po_3_ci_privacy_gate_baseline_scoping\",\n      \"category\": \"Governance / CI\",\n      \"title\": \"CI's 'Repository privacy gate' step fails unconditionally on every PR -- needs the same baseline-scoping fix already shipped for the local engineer hook\",\n      \"status\": \"planned\",\n      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n      \"target\": \"Follow-up to relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014 (GOV_PO_3_PUSH_HOOK_BASELINE_SCOPING, merged PR #127); dispatch via orchestrator start once picked up, no file overlap with orchestrator.py\",\n      \"note\": \"Found live 2026-09-08 while the Product Owner was monitoring five in-flight orchestrated movements: the GitHub Actions 'validation' workflow's 'Repository privacy gate' step (python main.py --repository-privacy-check, .github/workflows/validation.yml lines ~92 and ~172) scans the full working tree and fails unconditionally on the same two pre-existing, already-accepted, build-history-immutable false positives (project/build_history.json CREDENTIAL_LITERAL, relay/NXS-LOCAL-0003-local-relay-watch-command.json CREDENTIAL_LITERAL) -- confirmed failing identically on PR #127, #128, #129, #130, #131. relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014 already solved this exact problem shape for the LOCAL engineer pre-push hook (scripts/nexus_engineer_tool_gate.py, content-fingerprint baseline matching, not line-number matching) but that fix was never applied to the CI-level invocation of the same underlying scanner (application/cli.py's --repository-privacy-check path). There is no branch protection on this repo (confirmed via gh api repos/.../branches/main/protection -> 404 'Branch not protected'), so this has not blocked any merge so far -- engineers have been self-merging under the standing relay#13 authorization regardless of the red CI badge. Left unfixed, CI shows a permanent, uninformative red 'validate' status on every future PR, which defeats CI's own purpose: a genuinely new leak would look identical to this known noise. Fix direction (a full SESSION_START packet was already drafted 2026-09-08 at /private/tmp/nexus_po_ci_privacy_gate_packet.json before the Product Owner asked to hold dispatch for a new chat session): extract relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014's baseline-comparison logic into a shared module (utils/repository_privacy.py is the natural home) used by both the local hook and the CI entry point; CI's baseline is the PR's merge-base with origin/main (verify what ref info is actually available in this repo's GitHub Actions checkout for a pull_request event, and whether .github/workflows/validation.yml's checkout step needs a fetch-depth change to compute it); a finding present in both baseline and current scan is pre-existing debt and must not fail the gate, a finding only in the current scan still fails exactly as today. This movement's own PR will likely hit the same currently-broken CI check before its fix lands -- expected and self-resolving, same precedent as relay/NXS-LOCAL-0014's own SESSION_START noted.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app\",\n      \"category\": \"Orchestrator / Observability\",\n      \"title\": \"Interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench -- human-action inbox, live movement tracking, detail panel, message delivery, authorized command routing, integration visibility (supersedes the earlier read-only-dashboard framing)\",\n      \"status\": \"planned\",\n      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n      \"target\": \"Follow-up to relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013 (GOV_PO_3_ORCHESTRATOR_OBSERVABILITY, merged PR #129, terminal-only 'orchestrator watch'); AMENDED 2026-09-08 per Product Owner-provided design review (external architecture pass, 'Astra') before dispatch; explicitly requested by the Product Owner as the next dispatch once queued\",\n      \"note\": \"Product Owner feedback 2026-09-08, given directly after 'orchestrator watch' (relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013) shipped: the terminal table is 'cok yuzeysel bir arayuz' (too superficial an interface), followed by an explicit request to research the shape of this kind of app once and land on a complete, dispatch-ready spec rather than iterating back and forth. The spec below is that research, written so a single engineering movement can implement it without a further design round; treat it as strong direction, not a rigid contract -- the engineer session may adjust implementation detail, but should flag via RELAY_QUESTION rather than silently drop anything under 'REQUIRED'. NOT YET DISPATCHED -- the Product Owner asked to queue this rather than dispatch immediately, pending a session/chat handoff.\\n\\nPRODUCT OWNER'S EXPLICIT REQUIREMENTS (verbatim intent preserved): (1) a real clickable app, not a terminal redraw loop; (2) it reads the orchestrator's own state, and the refresh interval must be changeable from inside the UI itself (persisted, not only a --interval CLI flag); (3) a list of all tasks/movements; (4) clicking one opens a full detail view; (5) at a glance: what is currently being developed, what has already been developed (done/merged), and what stage each active movement is at, grouped active-on-top / recently-done-in-the-middle / closed-at-the-bottom, not one flat list.\\n\\nPRIOR ART SURVEYED (what comparable local dev-tool dashboards do, and what to borrow): CI/job dashboards (GitHub Actions run list, Airflow DAG-run UI, Prefect/Dagster run UI) all share the same shape -- a grouped/filterable run list with a colored status badge, a click-through detail view with a tabbed layout (overview / logs / timeline), and a live-tailing log pane. Process managers (pm2, Docker Desktop/Portainer, k9s) add the 'what's alive right now' angle: liveness derived from the actual OS-level process/container check, never from a timestamp alone -- confirms this movement's own finding that pid_alive, not heartbeat_at, is the only honest liveness signal today. The consistent architectural pattern across all of them for a single-user local tool: a thin local backend that already owns the ground truth (here: scripts/orchestrator.py's state dir + relay/*.json + each worktree's git status) exposes a small read JSON API; a static single-page frontend polls or subscribes to it; no user accounts, no multi-tenancy, bind to localhost only.\\n\\nRECOMMENDED ARCHITECTURE (REQUIRED unless the engineer session finds a concrete blocker, reported via RELAY_QUESTION): (A) Backend -- a new `python3 scripts/orchestrator.py dashboard [--port N, default e.g. 8765] [--relay-dir] [--state-dir]` subcommand, stdlib-only (http.server/socketserver/wsgiref -- matches the existing 'no new third-party dependency' invariant already enforced for orchestrator.py and local_relay.py), bound to 127.0.0.1 only. It serves (a1) one static HTML/CSS/JS page (vanilla, no build step, single file or a small handful under a new scripts/dashboard_assets/ or similar -- no npm/webpack/node toolchain in this repo, do not introduce one), and (a2) a small JSON API reusing the existing status-computation functions (_status_row, _read_last_activity, _heartbeat_age_seconds etc.) rather than re-implementing them: GET /api/movements (grouped list), GET /api/movements/<id> (detail: full .nexus/approved_task.json acceptance criteria, complete relay entry history in order with actor+marker+timestamp+body, git worktree status --porcelain parsed into a human label, ahead/behind origin/main counts, open PR number+state+CI conclusion via `gh pr list --head <branch>` if `gh` is available -- degrade gracefully to 'n/a' if not, never crash), GET /api/movements/<id>/log (tail of engineer.summary.log, or engineer.log if the summary doesn't exist yet, capped at a reasonable byte count), GET/PUT /api/config (currently just {\\\"poll_interval_seconds\\\": N}, persisted to a small JSON file e.g. .nexus/dashboard_config.json at the repo root -- not per-worktree). (B) Frontend refresh: plain polling via fetch() at the UI-configurable interval is sufficient and simplest (avoid SSE/WebSocket complexity unless the engineer session judges it trivial with stdlib alone -- do not add a dependency for this). (C) Real per-movement 'stage' string (addresses a gap this Product Owner already found in the existing terminal watch): derive from git worktree status instead of trusting heartbeat_at alone -- e.g. 'not started yet' (only SESSION_START posted, no worktree changes), 'developing' (uncommitted changes present), 'resolving merge conflict' (UU/AA porcelain markers), 'ready to push' (committed, no PR yet), 'PR open, CI running/red/green' (via gh), 'merged' (relay CLOSED + PR merged). (D) heartbeat_at itself: either make it genuinely periodic (the spawned engineer process or a lightweight orchestrator-side poller updates it on a real signal) or rename/relabel it honestly in both the API and the UI so it is never presented as liveness -- pid_alive stays the authoritative liveness check either way. (E) Grouping in the UI: active (phase running, pid_alive true) on top; done-but-recent (phase done, e.g. within the last N hours or last N items) in the middle; closed/merged in a collapsed-by-default bottom section -- exactly the three-tier grouping the Product Owner asked for. (F) Detail view tabs/sections: Overview (objective, acceptance criteria, current stage, branch, worktree path, PR link), Relay history (the full ordered marker log -- this is the audit trail GOV.PO.3 already relies on, so surfacing it in the UI is pure value, no new data), Log (live-tailing engineer.summary.log with fallback to raw engineer.log). (G) Read-only by default, mirroring 'orchestrator watch' and 'local_relay.py watch' precedent -- no start/stop/resume/merge action buttons in this first version; that would be a new write surface crossing back into the two-touchpoint governance boundary (GOV.PO.3 section on human interaction points) and should be its own, separately-decided follow-up if ever wanted, not bundled in silently here.\\n\\nOUT OF SCOPE for this movement (flag anything that looks like it needs these, do not build them here): authentication/multi-user support (single local user, no need); a build toolchain/npm dependency; write/action buttons (see (G)); mobile responsiveness beyond basic usability; historical analytics/charts beyond the three-tier list. ACCEPTANCE-SHAPE GUIDANCE for whoever turns this into a formal SESSION_START: reproduce the existing terminal watch's exact data shape as the JSON API's baseline (backward compatible, AC-5-style), add targeted tests for the new stage-derivation function and the config read/write endpoint in isolation (no need to test the frontend JS with a browser harness -- test the data/API layer, same testing philosophy relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013 used for its own watch loop).\\n\\n=== AMENDMENT 2026-09-08 (supersedes item (G) 'read-only, no action buttons' above; the rest of the original architecture research still stands as background) ===\\nProduct Owner-directed correction after external design review: a monitoring-only dashboard is not enough. What's actually needed is an interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench that surfaces what is expected of the Product Owner, routes the Product Owner's answer to the correct session, and lets the work continue from the same screen. Reading the 'two-touchpoint' principle (work selection, design decisions) as 'therefore read-only UI' was wrong -- work-selection and decision buttons ARE the interface for those exact two touchpoints, and a screen that lets the Product Owner change the poll interval persistently is already not purely read-only anyway. The scope correction below, written as the amendment to hand directly to the implementing engineer session (English, verbatim):\\n\\nAmend orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app from a monitoring-only dashboard to an interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench.\\n\\nPreserve the existing local stdlib backend and vanilla JS frontend. Reuse the orchestrator and local relay as the execution and decision sources; do not introduce another scheduler or independent state model.\\n\\nFirst delivery must include:\\n- A human-action inbox, ranked by blocked work and urgency.\\n- Concurrent movement cards with process status, explicit work stage, last observed activity, owner, and next action.\\n- Movement detail: acceptance criteria, conversation, events, diffs, tests, artifacts, worktree/session references, and preview links.\\n- Start/resume of authorized work, queued messages to the correct session, and observable message-delivery acknowledgments.\\n- Structured execution requests with action ID, exact operation, working directory, expected effects, and blocker source.\\n- Automatic routing of already-authorized engineering actions away from the PO role; ask the human only for an actual missing decision.\\n- Serialized integration visibility and existing authorized auto-merge.\\n\\nDo not infer work stage from Git dirtiness or heartbeat. Do not claim access to unavailable agent transcripts or hidden reasoning. UI actions must use existing authorization rules, reject stale requests, and prevent duplicate execution. Platform denials remain explicit.\\n\\nAcceptance demonstration: Track five concurrent movements; answer a pending design question; deliver a message to the correct worker; route an authorized command request; resume the affected work; open its produced UI/test artifact. Complete this flow without manual chat-to-chat copying or Git commands.\\n\\nDETAILED LAYOUT (from the same design review, informative -- the implementing session may adapt presentation but should preserve the five sections and their stated actions):\\nMain screen, five sections top to bottom: (1) 'Awaiting you' -- decisions, permission blocks, design questions; which work each one is blocking; actions: answer, approve an option, defer, reject. (2) 'Running movements' -- the concurrent movements, owner, real stage, last progress, next step; actions: open detail, send a message, request pause, stop. (3) 'PO agenda' -- suggested NEXT, ready work, dependencies, why each is waiting; actions: start, prioritize, queue. (4) 'Integration queue' -- PRs awaiting review/test/merge and why each is waiting; actions: view findings, request a fix. (5) 'Recently completed / archive' -- what shipped, test result, PR, remaining debt if any; actions: open the result and the artifact/UI that was built. A small summary strip above all five: e.g. '5 running - 2 awaiting decision - 1 in integration.' Any movement that needs a decision must surface at the TOP of its group, not just get sorted into 'active' generically.\\n\\nDetail panel (opens on click, right-hand pane), six tabs: Summary (what's being done, user-facing benefit, acceptance criteria, next step); Live activity (the agent's own posted progress notes, running command/test, latest result); Conversation (PO/engineer/reviewer messages, threaded, showing which message answered which); Changes (changed files, diff summary, branch/worktree, PR link); Evidence (test result, screenshot, render output, local UI preview); History (dispatch, correction, decision, retry, and integration events in order).\\n\\n'Where is Claude actually running' must be answerable via the relation agent/session -> movement -> worktree -> current activity -- surfaced explicitly, not hidden. If the platform does not expose it, never fabricate a full terminal transcript or hidden reasoning trace to fill the gap -- posted messages and tool events are what's real and what should be shown.\\n\\nThe single most valuable feature: an executable-request card. Convert a 'I can't run this in a terminal, no permission' message into a card shaped like: 'Test run blocked / Movement: .../ Requested action: .../ Target environment/worktree: .../ Expected effect: .../ Blocker source: PO role restriction / missing dependency / platform policy' with buttons [Route to engineer] [Run the authorized action] [Reject]. Three distinct cases must be told apart: (1) the action is already authorized but reached the wrong role -- the orchestrator routes it to the engineer automatically, no question surfaces to the Product Owner at all; (2) a genuinely new human decision is needed -- show the action and its effect, record the choice to the relay, let the affected work continue; (3) a platform/organizational policy is the actual blocker -- show the real blocker; a UI button must never be presented as if it can override that policy. The button must send a registered action id, not free-form shell text typed in the browser -- the backend resolves the id to its already-recorded operation, argument set, and working directory, which is what prevents running in the wrong worktree and prevents a double-click from starting the same thing twice; if the underlying command has changed since the id was issued, the stale approval must not apply.\\n\\nLive stage must never be inferred from git dirtiness alone: a dirty worktree does not mean 'writing code' and a clean one does not mean 'done'. Show three distinct fields -- Process status (running / exited / disconnected), Work stage (review / coding / testing / awaiting PO / integration), Last observed activity (e.g. 'test started 90s ago') -- with stage sourced from real orchestrator/engineer-posted events, heartbeat limited to meaning process reachability only, and 'last activity unknown' shown honestly when there is no evidence, never a fabricated progress percentage.\\n\\nA message box is itself part of the work: every movement needs a 'send a note to this work' field, and the message's own delivery state must be visible: Saved -> delivered to session -> received by agent -> answered. If delivery to the currently-running session isn't supported, queue it for the next continuation/resume call and say so explicitly in the UI -- never show 'Sent' for a message the agent never actually read.\\n\\nSecurity note carried over from the architecture review: once action buttons exist, localhost-only binding is not enough by itself -- add a session token plus Origin/CSRF checking, specifically so another web page open in the same browser cannot silently send commands to the local service without an extra confirmation screen.\\n\\nExplicitly deferred past the first delivery: a fully embedded terminal, desktop packaging, a drag-and-drop workflow editor, and advanced charting -- none of those block the acceptance demonstration above.\\n\\nSuccess criterion for this whole screen, stated by the Product Owner directly: 'I understood what work is waiting on me, I gave the answer it needed, and I saw the work continue on the same screen.'\\n\\n=== PO SCOPE BOUNDARY 2026-09-08 (decided, not merely flagged -- Product Owner asked for a resolved, dispatch-ready item that will not need another design round; consulting the external reviewer ('Astra') again remains an option later, but is not required to proceed) ===\\nThis backlog item's FIRST DISPATCH is bounded to the following. Anything not listed here is explicitly deferred (see 'DEFERRED' below), not silently included.\\n\\nIN SCOPE, decided:\\n- Read+relay-write only. Every card/button in this first delivery (decision inbox, message box, structured executable-request card) ends by appending an entry to the movement's own relay file (a RELAY_DECISION, a queued note, or an equivalent structured instruction) -- none of them execute git/gh/shell directly from the dashboard backend. The existing, already-audited engineer dispatch path (orchestrator start / resume, gated by scripts/nexus_engineer_tool_gate.py) is what actually executes anything, exactly as it does today for a PO decision typed into a relay append. This closes the authorization-duplication risk: there is exactly one place permission logic lives.\\n- 'Automatic routing of an already-authorized action' and the 'Run the authorized action' button are ONE mechanism, not two: both resolve to 'append this instruction to the relay, so the next dispatch/resume picks it up.' Build it once.\\n- The five-section main screen, six-tab detail panel, decision inbox ranked by urgency, movement cards with process-status/work-stage/last-activity as three distinct fields (never inferred from git dirtiness alone, never a fabricated progress percentage), and the structured executable-request card shape -- all as specified in the AMENDMENT section above -- are IN SCOPE for this first dispatch.\\n- Message-delivery UI must default its copy to the state that is actually achievable today: 'Saved -> queued for next resume' as the normal, expected outcome -- not 'delivered to session' or 'received by agent', which require a live session-control channel this repository does not have (confirmed: orchestrator.py's --resume is a fresh respawn into the same worktree, not a reconnect to a running claude session -- relay/NXS-LOCAL-0015's own finding, 2026-09-08). Do not build or imply a live-delivery guarantee.\\n- Local stdlib backend + vanilla JS frontend, orchestrator/relay as the only state and decision sources, localhost-bound. Session token + Origin/CSRF checking is IN SCOPE for this first dispatch specifically because relay-write buttons already exist here (writing a RELAY_DECISION is a real action, not merely a read) -- do not defer this to a later 'once we add real action buttons' phase; that phase is now.\\n\\nDEFERRED, explicitly out of scope for this dispatch (their own future backlog items if wanted, not part of this one):\\n- Any button that directly executes git/gh/shell from the dashboard backend itself (the earlier draft's 'Run the authorized action' as literal execution) -- stays relay-write-only per above until a specific, separately-authorized follow-up revisits it.\\n- The 'Evidence' tab's real content (screenshots, render output, local UI preview) -- ship the tab structurally (so the layout is future-proof) but it is allowed to show 'no artifact producer configured yet' rather than fabricated or empty-implying-broken content; wiring real screenshots/render output needs its own engineer-side output-contract addition (e.g. a documented .nexus/artifacts/ convention) first, not part of this dispatch.\\n- A fully embedded terminal, desktop packaging, drag-and-drop workflow editor, advanced charting (already deferred in the AMENDMENT above; restated here for a single source of truth).\\n- Any true live-session message channel (would require the orchestrator to persist and reconnect to a real claude session id -- a separate, larger movement if ever pursued; NOT bundled here).\\n\\nThis boundary is the final word for a first dispatch; the implementing engineer session should treat 'IN SCOPE' as build-it and 'DEFERRED' as report-if-tempted-to-build-it-anyway-via-RELAY_QUESTION, not as open questions requiring another Product Owner design round.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"orchestrator_background_task_exit_race\",\n      \"category\": \"Orchestrator / reliability\",\n      \"title\": \"An engineer's own claude -p background task can outlive its process's turn, leaving staged-but-uncommitted work when the session exits without waiting\",\n      \"status\": \"planned\",\n      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n      \"target\": \"scripts/orchestrator.py::_spawn_engineer and/or the ENGINEER_PROMPT -- observed live during relay/NXS-LOCAL-0018 (PR #133 merge-conflict completion), 2026-09-08\",\n      \"note\": \"Real bug, observed live, not hypothetical. relay/NXS-LOCAL-0018's engineer session staged its merge-conflict-resolution changes (`git add`), then ran the full pytest regression via the claude CLI's own internal background-task mechanism (the run exceeded a 120s foreground timeout and was auto-moved to the background, e.g. `Command did not complete within its 120s timeout and was moved to the background (ID: bhpt1icqs)`). The session's own turn then ended (it printed a final assistant message and returned `result (success)`) WITHOUT waiting for that background task's completion notification -- the orchestrator's process record then showed `pid_alive: false` with the relay still at `SESSION_START` (no RELAY_ACK/NOTE/QUESTION/CLOSE ever posted) and the worktree left with everything staged but not committed (`git status --short` showed all changes in the index, nothing in `git log`). Recovered this time by a plain `orchestrator start` resume, which happened to pick the work back up cleanly (no data loss), but this is not guaranteed in general -- a background task producing a result the next resumed session never sees, or resuming mid-way through an unverified test run, is a real correctness gap for the standing 'green tests -> self-merge' authorization (relay#13): a session must not be able to reach a commit/push/merge step on the strength of a background test run it never actually waited to see the result of.\\n\\nFix direction (not yet designed in detail -- needs its own investigation before a SESSION_START is written): (a) the ENGINEER_PROMPT (scripts/orchestrator.py) could be strengthened to explicitly instruct every engineer session to never treat a long-running validation command as backgroundable -- always run the full regression suite as a foreground, awaited command, even past whatever timeout the CLI would otherwise auto-background it at (this may need a per-invocation flag/env var to raise or disable that auto-background threshold for engineer sessions specifically, if one exists -- verify before assuming); (b) alternatively/additionally, the orchestrator's own resume path (decide_start) could detect this specific pattern (index has staged-but-uncommitted changes, relay never advanced past SESSION_START, pid dead) and inject an explicit instruction into the resumed session's context to first check for and finish any prior staged-but-uncommitted work and any background task results before proceeding, rather than relying on the resumed session to figure this out unprompted (it did, this time, but that's not yet a guarantee). Report via RELAY_QUESTION if investigation finds the CLI gives no way to control or query this background-task behavior from outside the session.\\n\\nNot urgent-blocking (recovery worked this time via a plain resume), but real and worth fixing before it causes an actual lost-work or premature-merge incident rather than just an extra resume cycle.\\n\\nPriority raised P1 -> P0 on 2026-09-08 per explicit Product Owner direction: this is the one concrete engineering item among this session's PO-orchestration-efficiency findings (see PRODUCT_DIRECTION_RECORD.md section 14), and the standing relay#13 'green tests -> self-merge' authorization must not remain exposed to an unverified background test result.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","numLines":34,"startLine":780,"totalLines":813}}}
