{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017xH5BxBKkWkPWD77iHUK9W","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"created_at\": \"2026-09-08T16:40:52Z\",\n3\t  \"entries\": [\n4\t    {\n5\t      \"actor\": \"po\",\n6\t      \"marker\": \"SESSION_START\",\n7\t      \"report\": {\n8\t        \"acceptance_criteria\": [\n9\t          \"AC-1: read project/backlog.json id orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app IN FULL before doing anything else. It is the complete, Product-Owner-decided bounded spec (prior-art survey, recommended architecture, the AMENDMENT into a PO+Orchestrator workbench, and the final 'PO SCOPE BOUNDARY' section with IN SCOPE / DEFERRED lists). This SESSION_START does not restate it -- treat the IN SCOPE section as build-it and DEFERRED as report-if-tempted-via-RELAY_QUESTION, not as open questions for another design round.\",\n10\t          \"AC-2: post one short technical-design RELAY_NOTE on this relay BEFORE writing any code, covering exactly three things the backlog note leaves as implementation detail: (a) the action-id registry design -- what identifies a registered relay-write action, how it stores the exact operation/argument-set/working-directory it resolves to, and how staleness is detected ('if the underlying command has changed since the id was issued, the stale approval must not apply'); (b) the relay-write schema each dashboard action appends -- RELAY_DECISION vs. a new lighter marker, justified against docs/design/LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md's existing marker set (do not invent an incompatible file format); (c) the session-token + Origin/CSRF scheme for the localhost-bound server. Wait for a Product Owner acknowledgment RELAY_NOTE/RELAY_DECISION on this relay before proceeding past this note -- the same two-phase pattern relay/NXS-LOCAL-0007 itself used (design reviewed once, then implementation), except here the design is scoped to these three technical questions only, not the whole feature (already decided in the backlog note).\",\n11\t          \"AC-3: once AC-2 is acknowledged, implement per the backlog note's IN SCOPE section: the five-section main screen (Awaiting you / Running movements / PO agenda / Integration queue / Recently completed), the six-tab detail panel (Summary / Live activity / Conversation / Changes / Evidence / History), movement cards with three distinct fields -- process status, work stage, last observed activity -- never inferred from git dirtiness alone and never a fabricated progress percentage, and the structured executable-request card (action id, exact operation, working directory, expected effect, blocker source, with the three-way routing: already-authorized -> auto-route to engineer with no PO question; genuinely new decision -> show and record to relay; platform/policy block -> show the real blocker, no button pretends to override it).\",\n12\t          \"AC-4: read+relay-write only, as decided in the backlog note's scope boundary -- every card/button ends by appending to the movement's own relay file (a RELAY_DECISION, a queued note, or an equivalent structured instruction); the dashboard backend never executes git/gh/shell directly. 'Automatic routing of an already-authorized action' and a 'run this' button are the SAME mechanism (append the instruction to the relay so the next dispatch/resume picks it up) -- build it once.\",\n13\t          \"AC-5: message-delivery UI copy defaults to 'Saved -> queued for next resume' as the normal, achievable outcome -- never implies a live session-control channel this repository does not have (orchestrator.py's --resume is a fresh respawn into the same worktree, not a reconnect to a running session -- relay/NXS-LOCAL-0015's own finding).\",\n14\t          \"AC-6: reuse scripts/orchestrator.py's existing status-computation functions and scripts/local_relay.py directly -- no new scheduler, no new state model, no third-party/npm dependency; stdlib backend (http.server/wsgiref), vanilla JS frontend, bound to 127.0.0.1 only, matching relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013's own precedent.\",\n15\t          \"AC-7: targeted tests for the new stage-derivation logic, the action-id registry/staleness check, and the config read/write endpoint, in isolation (no browser test harness needed -- test the data/API layer, same testing philosophy as relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013's own watch-loop tests).\",\n16\t          \"AC-8: acceptance demonstration per the backlog note: track several concurrent movements through the UI, answer a pending design question through it, deliver a message to the correct worker, route an authorized command request through it, resume the affected work, open its produced UI -- without manual chat-to-chat copying or git commands. Record real command/UI evidence (screenshots or terminal output), not a description.\",\n17\t          \"AC-9: validation and state: targeted tests green; because this is new scope touching scripts/orchestrator.py's status functions, run the full one-shot regression once (DEV.TEST.1); project-state bookkeeping in this PR.\"\n18\t        ],\n19\t        \"baseline\": {\n20\t          \"authority\": \"project/backlog.json id orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app is this movement's complete authority -- an external design review ('Astra'), a Product Owner counter-review, and a final Product-Owner-decided scope boundary, all dated 2026-09-08. docs/design/PRODUCT_DIRECTION_RECORD.md section 14 (reasoning-tier/dispatch-pacing defaults, also 2026-09-08) applies to how this movement itself is run if it ever needs to reference orchestrator dispatch behavior, though it dispatches no sub-work of its own.\",\n21\t          \"engineering_baseline\": \"DEV.3.3 (AUTOMATED_VALIDATED)\",\n22\t          \"product_baseline\": \"0.7.7 (AUTOMATED_VALIDATED)\",\n23\t          \"sequencing\": \"Independent of the CI-privacy-gate and background-task-exit-race movements dispatched alongside it -- new file surface (a dashboard subcommand + assets), no shared-file overlap.\"\n24\t        },\n25\t        \"context_not_loaded\": [\n26\t          \"everything outside orchestrator.py's status/state surface and the new dashboard code\"\n27\t        ],\n28\t        \"deployment_direction\": \"local validation only\",\n29\t        \"git\": {\n30\t          \"base\": \"origin/main\",\n31\t          \"lane\": \"feature/orchestrator-interactive-dashboard-app\"\n32\t        },\n33\t        \"invariants\": [\n34\t          \"Session token + Origin/CSRF checking is IN SCOPE from the first version, not deferred -- relay-write buttons are a real action, not merely a read\",\n35\t          \"No direct git/gh/shell execution from the dashboard backend -- relay-write only (AC-4)\",\n36\t          \"No fabricated progress percentage, liveness, or delivery-state data ever -- show 'unknown' honestly when there is no evidence\",\n37\t          \"No new scheduler, no new state model, no third-party/npm dependency (AC-6)\"\n38\t        ],\n39\t        \"merge_gate\": \"AC-2's design note needs an explicit Product Owner acknowledgment before AC-3 implementation begins (a design checkpoint, not a full stop on the whole movement). Once AC-3 onward is green, standing relay#13 applies: self-merge, report via RELAY_NOTE.\",\n40\t        \"movement_type\": \"ARCHITECTURE\",\n41\t        \"objective\": \"Build the interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench the Product Owner scoped and bounded in project/backlog.json id orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app -- a human-action inbox, live movement tracking with a real detail panel, message delivery, and authorized-command routing, all read+relay-write only.\",\n42\t        \"output_contract\": [\n43\t          \"A short technical-design RELAY_NOTE (AC-2), acknowledged before AC-3 begins\",\n44\t          \"scripts/orchestrator.py dashboard subcommand + backend + frontend assets (AC-3 through AC-6)\",\n45\t          \"Targeted tests (AC-7)\",\n46\t          \"Real acceptance-demonstration evidence (AC-8)\",\n47\t          \"Project-state bookkeeping in this PR\",\n48\t          \"SESSION_CLOSE on this relay file (absolute path)\"\n49\t        ],\n50\t        \"recommended_reasoning\": {\n51\t          \"reason\": \"New architecture on top of an already-fully-scoped backlog spec -- AC-2's three technical-design questions (action-id registry, relay-write schema, CSRF scheme) are genuine design decisions needing real reasoning; AC-3 onward is deterministic once AC-2 is acknowledged.\",\n52\t          \"tier\": \"Sonnet 5, extended thinking (high) for AC-2's design note; Sonnet 5, normal for AC-3 onward implementation\"\n53\t        },\n54\t        \"requirements\": [\n55\t          \"Do not restate or redesign the backlog note's already-decided IN SCOPE/DEFERRED boundary -- implement it\"\n56\t        ],\n57\t        \"risks\": [\n58\t          \"If AC-2's design questions turn out to need a genuinely new file format or dependency to answer well, stop and report via RELAY_QUESTION rather than silently expanding scope.\"\n59\t        ],\n60\t        \"scope\": {\n61\t          \"in\": [\n62\t            \"Everything listed IN SCOPE in the backlog note's 'PO SCOPE BOUNDARY 2026-09-08' section\",\n63\t            \"AC-2's three technical-design questions\"\n64\t          ],\n65\t          \"out\": [\n66\t            \"Everything listed DEFERRED in that same section -- direct git/gh/shell execution buttons, real Evidence-tab content, embedded terminal, desktop packaging, drag-and-drop editor, advanced charting, any true live-session message channel\"\n67\t          ]\n68\t        },\n69\t        \"validation_plan\": [\n70\t          \"Targeted tests (AC-7)\",\n71\t          \"Full one-shot regression once (AC-9)\",\n72\t          \"The real acceptance demonstration (AC-8)\",\n73\t          \"git diff --check\",\n74\t          \"privacy gate once at PR time\"\n75\t        ]\n76\t      },\n77\t      \"seq\": 1,\n78\t      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T16:40:52Z\"\n79\t    },\n80\t    {\n81\t      \"actor\": \"engineer\",\n82\t      \"marker\": \"RELAY_NOTE\",\n83\t      \"seq\": 2,\n84\t      \"subject\": \"AC-2 design note: action-id registry, relay-write schema, session-token/CSRF\",\n85\t      \"text\": \"(a) Action-id registry. An action_id is issued only when the backend renders an executable-request card (GET /api/movements/<id>). It is sha256(canonical_json({movement_id, relay_file, operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq}))[:16], where operation is one of {route_to_engineer, record_decision, reject} and args is the exact local_relay.py append parameters (marker/subject/text/next/authorized_by/scope/supersedes) the button will submit verbatim -- never free-form text typed in the browser. source_relay_seq = len(relay_obj[\\\"entries\\\"]) at issuance time, reusing the same signal orchestrator.py._status_row already computes for its REVISION_CHANGED_SINCE_DISPATCH check, rather than inventing a second staleness idea. Registry storage follows the existing runtime-bookkeeping pattern (outside the relay file, like orchestrator's own process records): one file per movement under NEXUS_ORCHESTRATOR_STATE_DIR, \\\"<movement_id>.actions.json\\\", mapping action_id to {operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq, issued_at, consumed_at, consumed_relay_seq}. On click: unknown id is 404; an already-consumed id is rejected (duplicate-click guard); the live relay file's current entry count is compared to source_relay_seq and a mismatch is rejected as stale (something was appended since the card was rendered -- a RELAY_CORRECTION or a newer decision). Only then does the backend call local_relay.py append with the registered args and record consumed_at/consumed_relay_seq from that call's result. local_relay.py's own content-hash optimistic-concurrency check (LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 6) remains a second, independent guard at the actual write, not replaced by this registry.\\n\\n(b) Relay-write schema. No new marker. LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 4 states the seven-marker vocabulary is exactly closed; adding an eighth would violate that already-written contract and this AC's own instruction not to invent an incompatible file format. Every dashboard action maps onto an existing marker: a genuinely new PO decision (AMENDMENT case 2) becomes RELAY_DECISION with authorized_by/scope/supersedes populated from the session (authorized_by = the bearer-token session's identity, scope = \\\"<movement_id> dashboard action\\\", supersedes = the prior entry's seq if this replaces one, else \\\"none\\\"); an already-authorized auto-route or a queued message-box note (case 1, and the \\\"send a note\\\" field) becomes RELAY_NOTE -- factual status/instruction, never itself a new authority; a reject on a genuinely-new-decision card becomes RELAY_DECISION recording the decline, since declining is still a PO decision. Case 3 (platform/policy block) gets no write button at all -- there is nothing to append, only the real blocker text the card already shows. The action_id/operation metadata rides as plain, greppable text inside subject (e.g. \\\"DASHBOARD_ACTION action_id=<id> op=<operation>\\\") rather than as new JSON fields, so no schema change is needed anywhere -- the engineer session already reads relay entries as free text today (the standing ENGINEER_PROMPT instruction to act on RELAY_CORRECTION/RELAY_DECISION entries), exactly like every other entry.\\n\\n(c) Session-token + Origin/CSRF. Reuses docs/design/OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 7.2's cookieless-bearer-token pattern directly, adapted to stdlib http.server since that document's fastapi/uvicorn stack is out of scope here per AC-6. The dashboard subcommand generates one secrets.token_urlsafe(32) per process launch, printed once to the terminal as http://127.0.0.1:<port>/#t=<token>; the frontend reads it from location.hash, strips it via history.replaceState, holds it only in memory, and sends it as an Authorization: Bearer header on every /api/* call. No cookie is ever set, so the ambient-credential class of CSRF is structurally impossible, the same reasoning that document already gives; Origin is still checked as defence in depth on every state-changing (PUT/POST) /api/* route -- rejected if present and not equal to http://127.0.0.1:<port> (absence is allowed, since the bearer token, not Origin, is the real authorization boundary). Server-side token comparison uses hmac.compare_digest, never ==. GET / and static assets stay unauthenticated (no evidence lives in them, matching that document's own rule); every /api/* route, read or write, requires the token. The token lives only in a module-level in-memory variable, regenerated on every dashboard invocation, never written to disk -- the same \\\"runtime bookkeeping, not durable governance history\\\" posture orchestrator.py's own state directory already uses.\\n\\nWaiting for acknowledgment before starting AC-3 implementation, per this movement's merge_gate.\",\n86\t      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T16:45:23Z\"\n87\t    },\n88\t    {\n89\t      \"actor\": \"po\",\n90\t      \"authorized_by\": \"Product Owner -- chat directive 2026-09-08 (this engineering session, direct instruction, not a nexus-po episode)\",\n91\t      \"marker\": \"RELAY_DECISION\",\n92\t      \"scope\": \"AC-2 design-note acknowledgment for movement ORCHESTRATOR_INTERACTIVE_DASHBOARD_APP only\",\n93\t      \"seq\": 3,\n94\t      \"subject\": \"AC-2 design acknowledged, AC-3 implementation authorized\",\n95\t      \"supersedes\": \"none\",\n96\t      \"text\": \"The action-id registry design (sha256-derived id keyed to movement/relay_file/operation/args/working_directory/source_relay_seq, staleness checked against live relay entry count), the relay-write schema choice (reusing RELAY_NOTE/RELAY_DECISION only, no new marker, per LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md's closed vocabulary), and the session-token/CSRF scheme (ephemeral bearer token via URL fragment, never persisted, hmac.compare_digest comparison, Origin checked as defense in depth) are all acknowledged and approved as designed. Proceed to AC-3 implementation per this movement's SESSION_START. No changes requested.\",\n97\t      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T17:06:49Z\"\n98\t    }\n99\t  ],\n100\t  \"id\": \"NXS-LOCAL-0021\",\n101\t  \"movement\": \"ORCHESTRATOR_INTERACTIVE_DASHBOARD_APP\",\n102\t  \"next_actor\": \"engineer\",\n103\t  \"refs\": [\n104\t    \"project/backlog.json\",\n105\t    \"scripts/orchestrator.py\",\n106\t    \"scripts/local_relay.py\",\n107\t    \"docs/design/LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md\",\n108\t    \"docs/design/GOV_PO_3_APPROVED_MOVEMENT_ORCHESTRATION.md\"\n109\t  ],\n110\t  \"schema_version\": 1,\n111\t  \"status\": \"AWAITING_ENGINEER\",\n112\t  \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-08T17:06:49Z\"\n113\t}\n114\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4a8dd4a7-31e7-434f-89b1-73b3ad9d3b22","uuid":"41d29003-8a00-4afd-acfc-c58d2f1d4f1e","timestamp":"2026-09-08T17:07:09.766Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/OzanDur/Codo/neXus/relay/NXS-LOCAL-0021-orchestrator-interactive-dashboard-app.json","content":"{\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-09-08T16:40:52Z\",\n  \"entries\": [\n    {\n      \"actor\": \"po\",\n      \"marker\": \"SESSION_START\",\n      \"report\": {\n        \"acceptance_criteria\": [\n          \"AC-1: read project/backlog.json id orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app IN FULL before doing anything else. It is the complete, Product-Owner-decided bounded spec (prior-art survey, recommended architecture, the AMENDMENT into a PO+Orchestrator workbench, and the final 'PO SCOPE BOUNDARY' section with IN SCOPE / DEFERRED lists). This SESSION_START does not restate it -- treat the IN SCOPE section as build-it and DEFERRED as report-if-tempted-via-RELAY_QUESTION, not as open questions for another design round.\",\n          \"AC-2: post one short technical-design RELAY_NOTE on this relay BEFORE writing any code, covering exactly three things the backlog note leaves as implementation detail: (a) the action-id registry design -- what identifies a registered relay-write action, how it stores the exact operation/argument-set/working-directory it resolves to, and how staleness is detected ('if the underlying command has changed since the id was issued, the stale approval must not apply'); (b) the relay-write schema each dashboard action appends -- RELAY_DECISION vs. a new lighter marker, justified against docs/design/LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md's existing marker set (do not invent an incompatible file format); (c) the session-token + Origin/CSRF scheme for the localhost-bound server. Wait for a Product Owner acknowledgment RELAY_NOTE/RELAY_DECISION on this relay before proceeding past this note -- the same two-phase pattern relay/NXS-LOCAL-0007 itself used (design reviewed once, then implementation), except here the design is scoped to these three technical questions only, not the whole feature (already decided in the backlog note).\",\n          \"AC-3: once AC-2 is acknowledged, implement per the backlog note's IN SCOPE section: the five-section main screen (Awaiting you / Running movements / PO agenda / Integration queue / Recently completed), the six-tab detail panel (Summary / Live activity / Conversation / Changes / Evidence / History), movement cards with three distinct fields -- process status, work stage, last observed activity -- never inferred from git dirtiness alone and never a fabricated progress percentage, and the structured executable-request card (action id, exact operation, working directory, expected effect, blocker source, with the three-way routing: already-authorized -> auto-route to engineer with no PO question; genuinely new decision -> show and record to relay; platform/policy block -> show the real blocker, no button pretends to override it).\",\n          \"AC-4: read+relay-write only, as decided in the backlog note's scope boundary -- every card/button ends by appending to the movement's own relay file (a RELAY_DECISION, a queued note, or an equivalent structured instruction); the dashboard backend never executes git/gh/shell directly. 'Automatic routing of an already-authorized action' and a 'run this' button are the SAME mechanism (append the instruction to the relay so the next dispatch/resume picks it up) -- build it once.\",\n          \"AC-5: message-delivery UI copy defaults to 'Saved -> queued for next resume' as the normal, achievable outcome -- never implies a live session-control channel this repository does not have (orchestrator.py's --resume is a fresh respawn into the same worktree, not a reconnect to a running session -- relay/NXS-LOCAL-0015's own finding).\",\n          \"AC-6: reuse scripts/orchestrator.py's existing status-computation functions and scripts/local_relay.py directly -- no new scheduler, no new state model, no third-party/npm dependency; stdlib backend (http.server/wsgiref), vanilla JS frontend, bound to 127.0.0.1 only, matching relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013's own precedent.\",\n          \"AC-7: targeted tests for the new stage-derivation logic, the action-id registry/staleness check, and the config read/write endpoint, in isolation (no browser test harness needed -- test the data/API layer, same testing philosophy as relay/NXS-LOCAL-0013's own watch-loop tests).\",\n          \"AC-8: acceptance demonstration per the backlog note: track several concurrent movements through the UI, answer a pending design question through it, deliver a message to the correct worker, route an authorized command request through it, resume the affected work, open its produced UI -- without manual chat-to-chat copying or git commands. Record real command/UI evidence (screenshots or terminal output), not a description.\",\n          \"AC-9: validation and state: targeted tests green; because this is new scope touching scripts/orchestrator.py's status functions, run the full one-shot regression once (DEV.TEST.1); project-state bookkeeping in this PR.\"\n        ],\n        \"baseline\": {\n          \"authority\": \"project/backlog.json id orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app is this movement's complete authority -- an external design review ('Astra'), a Product Owner counter-review, and a final Product-Owner-decided scope boundary, all dated 2026-09-08. docs/design/PRODUCT_DIRECTION_RECORD.md section 14 (reasoning-tier/dispatch-pacing defaults, also 2026-09-08) applies to how this movement itself is run if it ever needs to reference orchestrator dispatch behavior, though it dispatches no sub-work of its own.\",\n          \"engineering_baseline\": \"DEV.3.3 (AUTOMATED_VALIDATED)\",\n          \"product_baseline\": \"0.7.7 (AUTOMATED_VALIDATED)\",\n          \"sequencing\": \"Independent of the CI-privacy-gate and background-task-exit-race movements dispatched alongside it -- new file surface (a dashboard subcommand + assets), no shared-file overlap.\"\n        },\n        \"context_not_loaded\": [\n          \"everything outside orchestrator.py's status/state surface and the new dashboard code\"\n        ],\n        \"deployment_direction\": \"local validation only\",\n        \"git\": {\n          \"base\": \"origin/main\",\n          \"lane\": \"feature/orchestrator-interactive-dashboard-app\"\n        },\n        \"invariants\": [\n          \"Session token + Origin/CSRF checking is IN SCOPE from the first version, not deferred -- relay-write buttons are a real action, not merely a read\",\n          \"No direct git/gh/shell execution from the dashboard backend -- relay-write only (AC-4)\",\n          \"No fabricated progress percentage, liveness, or delivery-state data ever -- show 'unknown' honestly when there is no evidence\",\n          \"No new scheduler, no new state model, no third-party/npm dependency (AC-6)\"\n        ],\n        \"merge_gate\": \"AC-2's design note needs an explicit Product Owner acknowledgment before AC-3 implementation begins (a design checkpoint, not a full stop on the whole movement). Once AC-3 onward is green, standing relay#13 applies: self-merge, report via RELAY_NOTE.\",\n        \"movement_type\": \"ARCHITECTURE\",\n        \"objective\": \"Build the interactive PO + Orchestrator workbench the Product Owner scoped and bounded in project/backlog.json id orchestrator_interactive_dashboard_app -- a human-action inbox, live movement tracking with a real detail panel, message delivery, and authorized-command routing, all read+relay-write only.\",\n        \"output_contract\": [\n          \"A short technical-design RELAY_NOTE (AC-2), acknowledged before AC-3 begins\",\n          \"scripts/orchestrator.py dashboard subcommand + backend + frontend assets (AC-3 through AC-6)\",\n          \"Targeted tests (AC-7)\",\n          \"Real acceptance-demonstration evidence (AC-8)\",\n          \"Project-state bookkeeping in this PR\",\n          \"SESSION_CLOSE on this relay file (absolute path)\"\n        ],\n        \"recommended_reasoning\": {\n          \"reason\": \"New architecture on top of an already-fully-scoped backlog spec -- AC-2's three technical-design questions (action-id registry, relay-write schema, CSRF scheme) are genuine design decisions needing real reasoning; AC-3 onward is deterministic once AC-2 is acknowledged.\",\n          \"tier\": \"Sonnet 5, extended thinking (high) for AC-2's design note; Sonnet 5, normal for AC-3 onward implementation\"\n        },\n        \"requirements\": [\n          \"Do not restate or redesign the backlog note's already-decided IN SCOPE/DEFERRED boundary -- implement it\"\n        ],\n        \"risks\": [\n          \"If AC-2's design questions turn out to need a genuinely new file format or dependency to answer well, stop and report via RELAY_QUESTION rather than silently expanding scope.\"\n        ],\n        \"scope\": {\n          \"in\": [\n            \"Everything listed IN SCOPE in the backlog note's 'PO SCOPE BOUNDARY 2026-09-08' section\",\n            \"AC-2's three technical-design questions\"\n          ],\n          \"out\": [\n            \"Everything listed DEFERRED in that same section -- direct git/gh/shell execution buttons, real Evidence-tab content, embedded terminal, desktop packaging, drag-and-drop editor, advanced charting, any true live-session message channel\"\n          ]\n        },\n        \"validation_plan\": [\n          \"Targeted tests (AC-7)\",\n          \"Full one-shot regression once (AC-9)\",\n          \"The real acceptance demonstration (AC-8)\",\n          \"git diff --check\",\n          \"privacy gate once at PR time\"\n        ]\n      },\n      \"seq\": 1,\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T16:40:52Z\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"actor\": \"engineer\",\n      \"marker\": \"RELAY_NOTE\",\n      \"seq\": 2,\n      \"subject\": \"AC-2 design note: action-id registry, relay-write schema, session-token/CSRF\",\n      \"text\": \"(a) Action-id registry. An action_id is issued only when the backend renders an executable-request card (GET /api/movements/<id>). It is sha256(canonical_json({movement_id, relay_file, operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq}))[:16], where operation is one of {route_to_engineer, record_decision, reject} and args is the exact local_relay.py append parameters (marker/subject/text/next/authorized_by/scope/supersedes) the button will submit verbatim -- never free-form text typed in the browser. source_relay_seq = len(relay_obj[\\\"entries\\\"]) at issuance time, reusing the same signal orchestrator.py._status_row already computes for its REVISION_CHANGED_SINCE_DISPATCH check, rather than inventing a second staleness idea. Registry storage follows the existing runtime-bookkeeping pattern (outside the relay file, like orchestrator's own process records): one file per movement under NEXUS_ORCHESTRATOR_STATE_DIR, \\\"<movement_id>.actions.json\\\", mapping action_id to {operation, args, working_directory, source_relay_seq, issued_at, consumed_at, consumed_relay_seq}. On click: unknown id is 404; an already-consumed id is rejected (duplicate-click guard); the live relay file's current entry count is compared to source_relay_seq and a mismatch is rejected as stale (something was appended since the card was rendered -- a RELAY_CORRECTION or a newer decision). Only then does the backend call local_relay.py append with the registered args and record consumed_at/consumed_relay_seq from that call's result. local_relay.py's own content-hash optimistic-concurrency check (LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 6) remains a second, independent guard at the actual write, not replaced by this registry.\\n\\n(b) Relay-write schema. No new marker. LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md section 4 states the seven-marker vocabulary is exactly closed; adding an eighth would violate that already-written contract and this AC's own instruction not to invent an incompatible file format. Every dashboard action maps onto an existing marker: a genuinely new PO decision (AMENDMENT case 2) becomes RELAY_DECISION with authorized_by/scope/supersedes populated from the session (authorized_by = the bearer-token session's identity, scope = \\\"<movement_id> dashboard action\\\", supersedes = the prior entry's seq if this replaces one, else \\\"none\\\"); an already-authorized auto-route or a queued message-box note (case 1, and the \\\"send a note\\\" field) becomes RELAY_NOTE -- factual status/instruction, never itself a new authority; a reject on a genuinely-new-decision card becomes RELAY_DECISION recording the decline, since declining is still a PO decision. Case 3 (platform/policy block) gets no write button at all -- there is nothing to append, only the real blocker text the card already shows. The action_id/operation metadata rides as plain, greppable text inside subject (e.g. \\\"DASHBOARD_ACTION action_id=<id> op=<operation>\\\") rather than as new JSON fields, so no schema change is needed anywhere -- the engineer session already reads relay entries as free text today (the standing ENGINEER_PROMPT instruction to act on RELAY_CORRECTION/RELAY_DECISION entries), exactly like every other entry.\\n\\n(c) Session-token + Origin/CSRF. Reuses docs/design/OPERATOR_CONSOLE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 7.2's cookieless-bearer-token pattern directly, adapted to stdlib http.server since that document's fastapi/uvicorn stack is out of scope here per AC-6. The dashboard subcommand generates one secrets.token_urlsafe(32) per process launch, printed once to the terminal as http://127.0.0.1:<port>/#t=<token>; the frontend reads it from location.hash, strips it via history.replaceState, holds it only in memory, and sends it as an Authorization: Bearer header on every /api/* call. No cookie is ever set, so the ambient-credential class of CSRF is structurally impossible, the same reasoning that document already gives; Origin is still checked as defence in depth on every state-changing (PUT/POST) /api/* route -- rejected if present and not equal to http://127.0.0.1:<port> (absence is allowed, since the bearer token, not Origin, is the real authorization boundary). Server-side token comparison uses hmac.compare_digest, never ==. GET / and static assets stay unauthenticated (no evidence lives in them, matching that document's own rule); every /api/* route, read or write, requires the token. The token lives only in a module-level in-memory variable, regenerated on every dashboard invocation, never written to disk -- the same \\\"runtime bookkeeping, not durable governance history\\\" posture orchestrator.py's own state directory already uses.\\n\\nWaiting for acknowledgment before starting AC-3 implementation, per this movement's merge_gate.\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T16:45:23Z\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"actor\": \"po\",\n      \"authorized_by\": \"Product Owner -- chat directive 2026-09-08 (this engineering session, direct instruction, not a nexus-po episode)\",\n      \"marker\": \"RELAY_DECISION\",\n      \"scope\": \"AC-2 design-note acknowledgment for movement ORCHESTRATOR_INTERACTIVE_DASHBOARD_APP only\",\n      \"seq\": 3,\n      \"subject\": \"AC-2 design acknowledged, AC-3 implementation authorized\",\n      \"supersedes\": \"none\",\n      \"text\": \"The action-id registry design (sha256-derived id keyed to movement/relay_file/operation/args/working_directory/source_relay_seq, staleness checked against live relay entry count), the relay-write schema choice (reusing RELAY_NOTE/RELAY_DECISION only, no new marker, per LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md's closed vocabulary), and the session-token/CSRF scheme (ephemeral bearer token via URL fragment, never persisted, hmac.compare_digest comparison, Origin checked as defense in depth) are all acknowledged and approved as designed. Proceed to AC-3 implementation per this movement's SESSION_START. No changes requested.\",\n      \"timestamp\": \"2026-09-08T17:06:49Z\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"id\": \"NXS-LOCAL-0021\",\n  \"movement\": \"ORCHESTRATOR_INTERACTIVE_DASHBOARD_APP\",\n  \"next_actor\": \"engineer\",\n  \"refs\": [\n    \"project/backlog.json\",\n    \"scripts/orchestrator.py\",\n    \"scripts/local_relay.py\",\n    \"docs/design/LOCAL_RELAY_PROTOCOL.md\",\n    \"docs/design/GOV_PO_3_APPROVED_MOVEMENT_ORCHESTRATION.md\"\n  ],\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"status\": \"AWAITING_ENGINEER\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-09-08T17:06:49Z\"\n}\n","numLines":114,"startLine":1,"totalLines":114}}}
