{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Exit code 1\n463-      \"title\": \"Runtime Inventory Exclusion Policy Foundation\",\n464:      \"status\": \"done\",\n465-      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n466-      \"target\": \"DEV.0.4.1\",\n--\n582-      \"priority\": \"P2\",\n583-      \"target\": \"post OP.2 / DEPLOY.1A\",\n584-      \"note\": \"Design: docs/design/COMPLIANCE_CHECK_ENGINE.md sections 11-12. The product end-state is a write-capable device administration platform; the check model reserves an OPTIONAL remediation { primitive: <write_primitive_id>, parameters, verify_after: [steps] } block, unused and validator-rejected in CE.1-CE.3. CE.4 lets the engine, on an operator-authorised in-window run, execute the write primitive, re-run verify_after (reusing the CE.1 source/assert grammar verbatim), record an immutable audit entry, and auto-rollback on verify failure. A future REMEDIABLE sub-state on FINDING is additive. HARD-GATED on every prerequisite in docs/design/FAILOVER_ENGINE_ARCHITECTURE.md section 10 - the same bar as failover_controlled_execution / OP.2: mature VERIFY/TRACE/RECOVER, the DEPLOY.1 server, DEPLOY.1A OIDC + an RBAC OPERATE role + full audit, the CP device-interaction-safety audit, the network-device command gate for every read AND write primitive, and a signed change-management / safety review with the network-security leads. No implementation until all are met.\"\n585-    },\n586-    {\n587-      \"id\": \"remove_dormant_remote_cleanup\",\n588-      \"category\": \"Security / Code Health\",\n589-      \"title\": \"Remove dormant remote cleanup helper\",\n590:      \"status\": \"done\",\n591-      \"priority\": \"P0\",\n592-      \"target\": \"Local security hardening, before server exposure\",\n--\n600-      \"priority\": \"P1\",\n601-      \"target\": \"Local development; required before DEPLOY.1 viewer exposure\",\n602-      \"note\": \"AUTOMATED_VALIDATED 2026-08-31 (Sonnet 5, normal throughout). Implemented the frozen contract (docs/history/phase/FRONTEND_RENDERING_BOUNDARY.md) in the same session it was frozen in. AC-2 exhaustive audit reviewed all 97 static/app.js innerHTML sinks individually (not sampled) plus every shared helper they call -- zero gaps found, no fix required. AC-1 CSP <meta> tag added to templates/index.html; one implementation-time correction found and applied via real-Chromium console-error checking: frame-ancestors is spec-defined as unsupported via <meta> delivery, so it was removed (the other nine directives are unaffected). AC-3/AC-4/AC-5: new tests/test_frontend_rendering_boundary.py (5 tests) plus two hostile-label standalone devices added to tests/fixtures/uitest/unified.json -- static round-trip proof the raw hostile strings never appear as markup outside the JSON payload, and a real-Chromium (Playwright) proof the client's escapeHtml() renders them as inert text with zero elements created and zero dialogs fired. Evidence: full suite 888 passed / 23 skipped / 0 failed; repository privacy gate PASS/0; render harness green including the real-Chromium path. Manual browser check performed via real-Chromium Playwright automation across all seven modules (zero console errors, screenshots captured) rather than literal human interaction -- this sandbox has no display -- kept at automated_validated rather than done for that reason; a human interactive open on a real workstation remains a cheap, non-blocking follow-up.\"\n603-    },\n604-    {\n605-      \"id\": \"codebase_modularization\",\n606-      \"category\": \"Architecture / Code Health\",\n607-      \"title\": \"Behavior-preserving modularization of large frontend and workflow modules\",\n608:      \"status\": \"done\",\n609-      \"priority\": \"P1\",\n610-      \"target\": \"Local development, independent of server arrival\",\n--\n681-      \"priority\": \"P1 -- valuable evidence, requires a new command-gate row + readiness-contract mapping before implementation\",\n682-      \"target\": \"OP.0b follow-on (post S8 closure); implement only after PO approval of the gate row and the check mapping\",\n683-      \"note\": \"PO request 2026-09-04 (real-environment ClusterXL S8-A, operator-supplied evidence). VENDOR SEMANTIC: `cphaprob tablestat` prints the CCP 'Unique IP's Table' -- for each cluster interface, which members are currently seen exchanging CCP on it (local member rows plus peer member rows, with per-interface IP and MAC). It answers a question the approved battery cannot: `cphaprob -a if` (CP-A4) proves the LOCAL link state of each cluster interface, not that CCP traffic is actually flowing to the peer on it. Operator scenario: one cluster interface physically disconnected while its link reports UP -- `cphaprob -a if` and `cphaprob stat` still look healthy, but the peer's row for that interface disappears from tablestat; that is the precursor to a split-brain election. So tablestat is positive, per-interface, cross-member CCP reachability evidence. WHY NOT ADDED NOW: it is a NEW device command, which is a hard-stop condition of the S8 campaign (new command authority = PO gate, `OP_0B_1_COMMAND_GATE_PACKAGE.md`), and its readiness mapping is a readiness-contract decision, not a parser fix. Nothing in the approved battery changed; `cphaprob -a if` (A4) and `cphaprob -ia list` (A5) are already approved reads and both now parse the real output shapes. WHAT IMPLEMENTATION NEEDS: (1) a gate row modelled on CP-A4 -- Vendor Check Point; Action class CLASS_0_READ; Execution plane device-direct SSH, existing per-member persistent Expert shell; Shell/context Expert direct, physical/VS0; Expected calls per member 1; Concurrency/timeout/retry as CP-A4 (NO_RETRY); Session reuse yes, same session and same INTER_COMMAND_DELAY_SECONDS pacing; Read-only evidence yes (`cphaprob tablestat` is a ClusterXL monitoring command); Evidence category F (link/control health) and/or the split-brain category. (2) SAFE RETAINED FIELDS ONLY -- the raw table carries per-interface IP addresses and MAC addresses: the parser must retain NO address, NO MAC, NO interface name; only bounded counts/booleans: number of cluster interfaces observed, number on which BOTH members are seen, number on which the peer member is absent, and whether the local member is seen on every required interface (cross-referenced only by count against CP-A4's `Required interfaces:`). Structural skeleton diagnostic applies if the shape is unrecognised. (3) READINESS MAPPING (PO decision, not to be invented): candidate checks are `no_split_brain` (peer absent on a required cluster interface = CCP not exchanged = election risk) and `control_sync_link_health` (per-interface CCP reachability, stronger than link state). The classification of 'peer row absent on a required interface' -- FAIL versus INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE -- must be decided against the frozen 'never KNOWN_BAD from absence' law: here the absence is inside the device's own positively-reported table, which is a different thing from a read that produced nothing, but the contract must say so explicitly before the code does. (4) VSX: physical/VS0 context only unless a per-VS semantic is separately established. (5) Tests: parser fail-closed on unknown shape; value-free retained fields (no IP/MAC survives); one call per member in the same shell; battery order and S4 command authority unchanged. Until this item is implemented, the readiness contract keeps its current (weaker) control-link evidence: link state, not CCP exchange.\"\n684-    },\n685-    {\n686-      \"id\": \"ha_entity_operational_lock\",\n687-      \"category\": \"Safety / Architecture\",\n688-      \"title\": \"Per-HA-entity operational lock -- the CLASS 2 lock grain does not exist\",\n689:      \"status\": \"done\",\n690-      \"priority\": \"P0 -- before CLASS 2\",\n691-      \"target\": \"OP.2.B (architecture FROZEN 2026-09-04 in docs/history/phase/OP_2_0_CONTROLLED_HA_OPERATION_ARCHITECTURE.md)\",\n(eval):1: == not found","is_error":t